      relevantPassage: 'The court held that administrators are legal representatives of a deceased person’s interests, but they do not automatically become company members merely because they possess letters of administration. A further court order is required. The court answered the first issue in their favour but refused the second and third requests because insufficient information had been provided about when the other shareholders became untraceable. The application was therefore partly granted, with no order as to costs.',
      fullTextSummary: 'The petitioners were administrators of the estate of Arnaldo Amadori, a deceased shareholder and managing director of Buyuni Company Limited. Other shareholders and directors were reportedly foreigners who had become untraceable. As a result, the company had no resident directors and could not properly manage its property, file returns with BRELA, comply with tax requirements or conduct its affairs. The petitioners asked the High Court to recognize them as company members representing the deceased’s shares, authorize them to update the register of members and permit them to notify the Registrar of Companies. The court held that administrators are legal representatives of a deceased person’s interests, but they do not automatically become company members merely because they possess letters of administration. A further court order is required. The court answered the first issue in their favour but refused the second and third requests because insufficient information had been provided about when the other shareholders became untraceable. The application was therefore partly granted, with no order as to costs.',

