      // 9. Court's Reasoning
      courtReasoningPoints: [
        'First Issue (Representation): Under Section 99 of the Probate and Administration of Estates Act and Table A Regulations 26–28, administrators legally represent the deceased.',
        'However, obtaining letters of administration does not automatically make the administrator a registered company member; a further court order is required.',
        'The first issue was therefore answered in the affirmative in favour of the petitioners.',
        'Second & Third Issues (Untraceable Shareholders): Winding up may be appropriate when members no longer wish to continue business, but is harmful where the company remains a going concern and members want business to continue.',
        'The petition did not state precisely when Donatella and Andrea Amadori became untraceable.',
        'The court referred to a requirement involving an untraceability period of at least six years.',
        'The evidence provided was therefore insufficient to grant the second and third requests.',
        'The court observed that BRELA and the untraceable shareholders should properly be joined as parties in proceedings of this nature.'
      ],
      courtReasoningSources: 'Sources: pages 7–14.',
      reasoning: 'The High Court reasoned that administrators legally represent a deceased shareholder under Section 99 of Cap. 352 and Table A Regulations 26–28, but letters of administration do not confer automatic company membership without a specific court order. On the requests to update the register and notify the Registrar without the other foreign shareholders, the court observed that winding up should not be used when a viable company can continue, but the petitioners failed to provide evidence establishing that the untraceable shareholders had been missing for at least six years. Consequently, only the representative declaration was granted.',

