      relevantPassage: 'The Court of Appeal found that the disputed Will had never been tendered or admitted as evidence. The High Court therefore acted unlawfully by relying on it. The High Court also failed to make a clear final determination allowing or dismissing the caveat before deciding the petition. These irregularities rendered the contentious trial a nullity. The Court of Appeal nullified the proceedings and appointment of administrators and ordered a retrial before another judge.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Ritha John Makala and Ngana Andrew Mziray petitioned for letters of administration over the estate of their father, Dr. Tito Mziray Andrew, alleging that he died intestate. Chantal Tito Mziray, the deceased’s widow, and Enock Andrew Mziray entered a caveat claiming that the deceased had left a valid Will appointing Chantal as trustee and executor. The respondents disputed the authenticity and validity of that Will. The High Court treated the contested probate proceedings as a civil suit, declared the Will invalid and appointed Ritha, Ngana and Enock as joint administrators. The Court of Appeal held that the disputed Will was never tendered or admitted as evidence and an unadmitted attachment cannot form the basis of a judgment. Furthermore, the High Court failed to expressly determine the caveat before appointing administrators. The Court of Appeal nullified the trial proceedings and appointments and ordered a retrial before another judge.',

