      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that merely entering information into a computer and printing an invoice does not necessarily make the invoice a computer-generated electronic record. The electronic-evidence objection was therefore rejected. The court also found that copies acknowledged by the defendant in its Written Statement of Defence and list of documents were admissible. However, ten documents that had not been pleaded were excluded. The objections were consequently partly sustained.',
      fullTextSummary: 'During the trial of a commercial dispute in Civil Case No. 195 of 2015, the plaintiff’s witness, Hamis Diriye Abdi (PW1), attempted to tender commercial records (invoices, delivery notes, release orders, transaction lists, and loading documents). The defendant raised two main objections: (1) that the invoices were computer-generated records requiring electronic certification under section 18 of the Electronic Transactions Act 2015; and (2) that several documents were uncertified photocopies/secondary evidence violating section 66 of the Tanzania Evidence Act. The High Court (Y.J. Mlyambina, J.) held that merely typing or printing an invoice from a computer does not convert it into a data message or electronic transaction record requiring section 18 certification. Furthermore, photocopies expressly acknowledged in the defendant’s Written Statement of Defence and list of documents were admissible under section 67(1)(b) of the Evidence Act. However, ten documents that had not been pleaded were excluded. The defendant’s objections were partly sustained, costs to follow the event, and the main commercial suit remained pending.',

