      relevantPassage: 'The Court explained that granting an extension of time is discretionary and depends upon the circumstances of each case. Although an applicant is normally required to account for every day of delay, the Court found that Kawana had been continuously attempting to pursue his appeal. He was described as being in the “court corridor tirelessly pursuing” the matter. The earlier proceedings and the defective appeal constituted a technical delay. The Court found no deliberate inaction or negligence and concluded that the respondents would not suffer material prejudice if the application was granted.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Leonard Kawana applied for an extension of time to file a land appeal against the decision of the District Land and Housing Tribunal for Mbeya in Land Application No. 153 of 2010. The High Court (D.B. Ndunguru, J.) found that the applicant had continuously attempted to pursue his appeal: part of the delay resulted from waiting seven months for copies of court records from the tribunal, and another part arose after his earlier Land Appeal No. 19 of 2017 was struck out due to argumentative grounds. The court held that time spent pursuing earlier defective proceedings constitutes a technical delay rather than culpable negligence. Finding that the applicant had been tirelessly pursuing his rights in the court corridors without deliberate delay, and that the respondents would suffer no material prejudice, the High Court granted the application and allowed 45 days from 23 December 2020 to file the intended appeal, with no order as to costs.',

