      relevantPassage: 'The High Court decided that the property had been acquired during the deceased’s polygamous marriage and was therefore matrimonial property. Its transfer to the company without spousal consent was declared null and void. The Court distinguished between a matrimonial home and broader matrimonial property, holding that commercial property can be matrimonial property even when the spouses never occupied it as their residence. However, the property remained subject to the ongoing probate proceedings concerning the deceased’s estate.',
      fullTextSummary: 'The plaintiffs, Martina Martin Silayo and Emilia Martin Lyakituru (Amen Martin Shao), widows of the late Martin Alex Silayo, sued Godfrey Martin Silayo and 12 others (including Martin & Sons Company Limited) claiming ownership of commercial premises on Plot No. 6, Block I, Section III, Market Street, Moshi Municipality (Title No. 056041/54). The deceased had transferred the property to Martin & Sons Co. Ltd in 2013 for a nominal consideration of one shilling without obtaining spousal consent. The High Court (S.B. Mkapa, J.) held that matrimonial property is broader than a matrimonial home and encompasses commercial property acquired during a polygamous marriage. Sole registration in the deceased’s name did not extinguish the wives’ beneficial interests, and prior spousal consent given for a CRDB loan recognized its matrimonial nature. The transfer to the company without spousal consent violated Section 59(1) of the Law of Marriage Act and was declared null and void. The property was declared matrimonial property belonging to the deceased and his three wives, subject to ongoing probate proceedings. Non-land claims (bank funds, vehicles, mesne profits) were dismissed for lack of proof and jurisdiction, with each party bearing its own costs.',

