      courtReasoningSources: 'Sources: judgment pages 6–11.',
      reasoning: 'The High Court (S.B. Mkapa, J.) ruled that matrimonial property is not restricted to the residential roof over the family’s heads (the matrimonial home) but extends to commercial real estate acquired during the subsistence of the marriage. The disputed commercial building was acquired and developed during the deceased’s polygamous marriage with direct and indirect spousal contributions. The deceased previously acknowledged this by procuring spousal consent for a CRDB loan. Consequently, the purported transfer to Martin & Sons Company Limited for a token consideration of one shilling without spousal consent was in direct contravention of Section 59(1) of the Law of Marriage Act and void ab initio. The property was declared matrimonial property of the deceased and his three wives, to be administered under the pending probate cause. Non-land prayers were dismissed.',

