      relevantPassage: 'Because the statutory time limit had expired, the District Court lacked jurisdiction. Section 22(4) of the Magistrates’ Courts Act limits the District Court’s revisional jurisdiction over Primary Court proceedings to 12 months from the determination of those proceedings. Because approximately 22 months and 12 days had passed, the District Court acted outside the statutory period. Its proceedings, ruling and drawn order were consequently quashed and set aside.',
      fullTextSummary: 'The applicants, Stephen Samwel Kimbele and Christopher Samwel Kimbele, challenged Civil Revision No. 06 of 2018 determined by the District Court of Arusha which originated from Primary Court proceedings. They argued that the District Court revision was instituted approximately 22 months and 12 days after the Primary Court decision, far exceeding the 12-month statutory time limit under Section 22(4) of the Magistrates’ Courts Act (Cap. 11), thus depriving the District Court of jurisdiction. Counsel for the respondents, Mr. Moses Mahuna, formally conceded that the revision was time-barred. The High Court (M.R. Gwae, J.) held that jurisdiction is a creature of statute and cannot be assumed or conferred by consent where the law explicitly bars it. The application for revision was allowed, and the District Court proceedings, ruling, and drawn order were quashed and set aside in their entirety. No separate order as to costs was made.',

