      relevantPassage: 'The High Court found that the trial magistrate merely summarized the evidence but failed to analyse and evaluate the prosecution and defence evidence. The trial judgment therefore failed to comply with Section 312 of the Criminal Procedure Act. The appeal was allowed without determining whether the appellants committed the offence. The case was returned to the trial court for a magistrate with competent jurisdiction to recompose a proper judgment after evaluating all the evidence.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Emmanuel Martin and John Waziri appealed against their convictions and 30-year prison sentences for armed robbery involving the theft of a SAR firearm from a prison officer at Songwe Prison in Mbeya (Criminal Case No. 91 of 2013). The High Court (J.M. Karayemaha, J.) found that the trial magistrate merely summarized and narrated the testimony of witnesses without analysing, evaluating, or weighing the prosecution and defence evidence, violating the mandatory judgment-writing requirements of Section 312 of the Criminal Procedure Act (Cap. 20). Holding that trial courts are uniquely positioned to assess witness credibility and demeanour, the High Court declined to re-evaluate the record itself, allowed the appeal on procedural grounds without determining guilt or innocence, and remitted the case back to the trial court for a competent magistrate to recompose a proper judgment evaluating all evidence. Dissatisfied parties retain the right to appeal the recomposed judgment.',

