      relevantPassage: 'The High Court expunged the PF3 because it had been tendered by a witness who was neither its author nor a medical expert. Nevertheless, the Court held that a sexual offence may be proved through credible testimony from the victim even without a PF3. The victim knew the appellant before the incident, identified him during daylight and named him shortly afterwards. Minor discrepancies did not affect the central allegation or destroy the prosecution’s case. The appeal was dismissed, and both convictions and sentences (30 years and 3 years running concurrently) were upheld.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Jeremiah Johson @ Mosha appealed against his convictions and concurrent sentences of 30 years’ imprisonment for an unnatural offence (Section 154(1) Penal Code) and 3 years for assault causing actual bodily harm (Section 241 Penal Code) arising from an incident on 6 April 2019 at Ashira Marangu in Moshi District. The appellant challenged the change of trial magistrate under Section 214 CPA, his trial in absentia after jumping bail under Section 226 CPA, statutory defects in the judgment under Section 312(2) CPA, the improper tendering of the PF3 medical report by an unqualified witness, and alleged evidentiary contradictions. The High Court (S.H. Simfukwe, J.) expunged the PF3 medical report because PW4 was not its author or a medical professional capable of answering technical questions. However, the court held that sexual offences can be established without medical evidence where victim testimony is credible and corroborated. The victim knew the appellant prior to the crime, had ample daylight observation, promptly reported the attack, and displayed physical distress corroborated by other witnesses. Minor contradictions did not affect the substratum of the charge, and the failure to specify sections in the judgment was curable under Section 388 CPA without prejudice. The appeal was dismissed in its entirety and all convictions and sentences were affirmed.',

