      // 9. Court's Reasoning
      courtReasoningPoints: [
        'Change of Magistrate: The change occurred because the trial magistrate was transferred, and the reason was recorded. Discrepancies between typed and handwritten minutes were typographical; handwritten records confirmed proper continuation under Section 214 CPA.',
        'Trial in Absentia: The appellant voluntarily jumped bail after PW2 testified. Upon rearrest before judgment, he was given full opportunity to explain his absence, which was rejected as baseless. No denial of the right to be heard occurred under Section 226 CPA.',
        'Curable Judgment Defect: Failure to explicitly cite the sections in the final judgment was curable under Section 388 CPA since the appellant was fully appraised of the charges and suffered no prejudice.',
        'Expungement of PF3: PW4 was a police officer, not the author or a medical expert capable of answering medical questions. The PF3 was improperly admitted and expunged.',
        'Sexual Offence Proved Without PF3: A sexual offence may be proved through credible victim testimony alone. The victim knew the appellant, saw him in broad daylight, named him immediately, and exhibited physical distress and injury observed by witnesses.',
        'Minor vs Material Discrepancies: Minor inconsistencies regarding exact pain location and reporting timing did not destroy the core prosecution case.'
      ],
      courtReasoningSources: 'Sources: judgment pages 5–15.',
      reasoning: 'The High Court (S.H. Simfukwe, J.) held that the procedural challenges to the trial lacked merit. The change of magistrate complied with Section 214 CPA, and the appellant’s trial in absentia was lawfully conducted under Section 226 CPA after he jumped bail. Although the PF3 medical report was expunged because it was tendered by a non-expert police witness (PW4), the law in Tanzania firmly establishes that sexual offences and assault can be proved through credible victim testimony without medical reports. The victim had known the appellant previously, identified him during a daylight encounter, and was corroborated by witnesses who saw her physical trauma and injuries immediately afterwards. Minor discrepancies did not undermine the prosecution case.',

