      relevantPassage: 'The High Court found serious weaknesses in the prosecution’s case. The motorcycle registration card and sale agreement were admitted but never read aloud in court. They were therefore expunged from the record. The prosecution also failed to call Michael Paschal, the person who allegedly informed the complainant that the motorcycle had been stolen, justifying an adverse inference. Additionally, the trial magistrate failed to consider the appellant’s defence that the criminal case had been fabricated because of an existing family land dispute. These defects created reasonable doubt. The appeal was allowed, the conviction quashed and the five-year sentence set aside.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Jonas Paschal appealed against his conviction and 5-year prison sentence for stealing by agent (Section 273(b) Penal Code) concerning a SANLG motorcycle (Reg. MC 320 BFR valued at TZS 1,300,000) allegedly entrusted to him by his brother, Noah Paschal, for bodaboda commercial passenger transport. The High Court (A.Y. Mwenda, J.) found fatal evidentiary defects in the prosecution case: (1) Exhibit P1 (registration card) and Exhibit P2 (sale agreement) were admitted without being read aloud or explained in open court, necessitating their expungement; (2) The prosecution failed to call Michael Paschal, the key informant who reported the alleged theft, warranting an adverse inference under Section 122 of the Evidence Act; and (3) The trial court ignored the appellant’s defence that the criminal complaint was a fabrication motivated by a pre-existing family land dispute pending before the Katahoka Village Government. Re-evaluating the evidence, the High Court held that these cumulative defects created reasonable doubt entitled to benefit the appellant under Section 114(1) of the Evidence Act. The appeal was allowed, the conviction quashed, and the 5-year sentence set aside.',

