      courtReasoningPoints: [
        'Unread Exhibits Expunged: Exhibits P1 (registration card) and P2 (sale agreement) were admitted but never read aloud in open court, constituting a fatal defect under Anania Clavery and Hassan Said Twalib; both were expunged.',
        'Oral Evidence Weakness: Without documentary exhibits, entrustment and ownership rested solely on verbal testimony of husband and wife (PW1 and PW2) without independent corroboration.',
        'Adverse Inference for Missing Witness: Michael Paschal, the person who allegedly discovered and broke news of the theft, was a necessary witness. Failure to call him justified an adverse inference under Section 122 Evidence Act.',
        'Failure to Analyse Defence: The trial magistrate summarized the appellant’s defence of a family land dispute at Katahoka Village but failed to evaluate it, assuming without evidence that Noah acted out of brotherly love.',
        'Reasonable Doubt: The combination of expunged exhibits, missing material witness, lack of written entrustment, and unexamined family dispute created reasonable doubt that benefited the appellant.'
      ],
      courtReasoningSources: 'Sources: judgment pages 5–12.',
      reasoning: 'The High Court (A.Y. Mwenda, J.) found that the trial was plagued by fatal procedural and substantive shortcomings. First, Exhibits P1 and P2 were admitted without being read aloud in court as required by established precedent, leading to their expungement. Second, the prosecution inexplicably failed to summon Michael Paschal, the critical witness who purportedly informed Noah that the motorcycle had vanished, creating a gap that triggered an adverse inference. Third, the trial magistrate failed to scrutinize the appellant’s defence regarding a bitter pre-existing family land dispute at Katahoka Village that provided a strong motive for false accusation. Taking all these weaknesses together, the prosecution failed to prove stealing by agent beyond reasonable doubt.',

