      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that Section 22 of the Magistrates’ Courts Act does not prescribe one mandatory format for initiating revision. A person may move the District Court by formal application, a complaint letter, an oral complaint, or the court may act suo motu. A complaint letter is not invalid merely because it does not cite the enabling statutory provision. Furthermore, the District Court erred in deciding an unargued ground on its own without hearing the parties. The appeal was allowed and the revision was remitted to be heard on its merits.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Julieth Jackson Severine and Kashaija Jackson Severine appealed against the District Court ruling in Revision Application No. 3 of 2020 which struck out their revision request challenging Fredrick Kaiza Bifanimanya’s appointment as administrator of the estate of the late Jackson Severine (Uyole Primary Court Probate Cause No. 77 of 2019). The District Court struck out the revision because the appellants’ written complaint letter failed to cite Section 22 of the Magistrates’ Courts Act (Cap. 11). The High Court (A.A. Mbagwa, J.), hearing the appeal ex parte following substituted service on the respondent, allowed the appeal and quashed the District Court’s ruling. The High Court established that Section 22(1) MCA provides broad revisional power without prescribing a rigid procedural format: revision of Primary Court proceedings may be initiated via formal chamber summons with affidavit, a written complaint/letter, an oral complaint, or by the District Court acting suo motu. While formal court applications should cite enabling provisions, ordinary complaint letters are not invalidated by non-citation. Furthermore, the District Court violated natural justice by raising and determining the non-citation point suo motu without giving the parties an opportunity to be heard. Revision Application No. 3 of 2020 was ordered to be heard on its merits.',

