      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that in sexual offences, the victim’s testimony is normally the best and most crucial evidence, provided it passes the test of truthfulness. Here, the victim’s account was credible and directly supported by an eyewitness who responded to screams and found the appellant with the victim at the scene, as well as by medical examination. Differences concerning the victim’s residence, travel route and crime scene did not create a defective charge, and apparent consent procured through force falls squarely under Section 130(2)(b) of the Penal Code. The appeal was dismissed and the 30-year sentence affirmed.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Juma Idd Yohana @ Sita appealed against his conviction and statutory 30-year prison sentence for rape under Sections 130(1), 130(2)(b), and 131(1) of the Penal Code (Cap. 16) entered by the District Court in Criminal Case No. 104 of 2020. The appellant challenged the judgment on grounds of variance in crime scene location (Ruvu Jiungeni in charge vs Ruvu Mferejini residence / Kwa Sita route), improper invocation of Section 130(2)(b), improbability of the victim’s narrative, minor contradictions regarding his transfer to police custody, and the prosecution’s failure to call the village chairman, militiamen and investigating officer. The High Court (S.H. Simfukwe, J.) dismissed the appeal, holding that: (1) Ruvu Mferejini was merely the victim’s home and Kwa Sita her departure point, which did not contradict the Ruvu Jiungeni locus in the charge; (2) The victim pretended to yield solely to ease the appellant’s grip to scream, falling squarely within Section 130(2)(b) (consent vitiated by force/intimidation); (3) The victim’s cogent testimony was strongly corroborated by an eyewitness (Simon Samwel) who caught the appellant at the scene and by the clinical officer who examined her; (4) Under Section 143 of the Evidence Act, no particular number of witnesses is required, and the non-calling of peripheral officials was not fatal; and (5) The defence of a family land dispute was an uncorroborated afterthought not put to the victim in cross-examination. Conviction and 30-year sentence were confirmed.',

