      relevantPassage: 'The High Court delivered a landmark 66-page ruling holding that: (1) The Commission for Mediation and Arbitration (CMA) is a quasi-judicial body and not a "court" under the Government Proceedings Act; (2) The Government and Attorney General cannot be sued before the CMA without express statutory authority; (3) A 90-day notice under the Government Proceedings Act does not extend or suspend the specific 30-day limitation period for referring termination disputes to the CMA; (4) Time-barred disputes should be dismissed rather than struck out; (5) Failure to file CMA Form No. 10 under Regulation 34(1) before seeking revision is a fatal defect rendering the revision incompetent; and (6) The High Court invoked its own revisional powers to nullify the CMA proceedings for lack of jurisdiction.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Mlenga Kalunde Mirobo, a former driver employed by Tanzania National Parks (TANAPA), challenged the termination of his employment. After his internal appeal was rejected by the Director General, he issued a 90-day notice of intention to sue under Section 6 of the Government Proceedings Act (Cap. 5) and subsequently referred his unfair termination dispute to the CMA (Labour Dispute No. CMA/IR/03/2021). The CMA struck out the dispute for being referred outside the statutory 30-day labour limitation period under Section 86(1) of the Employment and Labour Relations Act (Cap. 366). The applicant sought labour revision before the High Court without first filing CMA Form No. 10 (notice of intention to apply for revision). In a comprehensive 66-page ruling, the High Court (Y.J. Mlyambina, J.) held: (1) The CMA is a quasi-judicial administrative body, not a "court" within the meaning of the Government Proceedings Act; (2) The Attorney General and Government (including TANAPA as a public body) cannot be sued in the CMA without express statutory grant; (3) Serving a 90-day notice under Cap. 5 does not extend the separate 30-day CMA referral window (the specific labour limitation prevails under lex specialis); (4) Hopelessly time-barred claims must be dismissed under Section 3 of the Law of Limitation Act, not merely struck out; (5) Public servants must exhaust statutory public-service appeal machinery and seek judicial review rather than filing fresh CMA disputes; (6) Failure to file mandatory CMA Form No. 10 rendered the revision application incompetent; and (7) Although the applicant’s revision had no merit, the High Court exercised its inherent and statutory revisional powers under Rule 28(1) of the Labour Court Rules to nullify the entire CMA proceedings for complete want of jurisdiction.',

