      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that an application for a temporary injunction pending the determination of a main land suit is premature and incompetent where the main case has already been ordered to proceed ex parte against the applicants and their separate application to set aside that ex parte order remains pending and unresolved. Furthermore, a third party who is not a defendant to the main land case cannot properly be joined as a respondent to an interlocutory injunction in that suit. The application was struck out with costs without reaching its substantive merits.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Nazareno Makilika and Avelina Makilika applied for a temporary injunction under Section 68(c) and Order XXXVII of the Civil Procedure Code (Cap. 33) to restrain Hamisa Abdallah Muhsin, Nyanya Mohamed Muhsin, and Najeeb Yeslam Saeed from dealing with disputed land pending the determination of Land Case No. 200 of 2020. In the main land suit, an order had already been issued directing the case to proceed ex parte against the applicants for failure to file a defence. The applicants had lodged a separate application (Misc. Land Application No. 369 of 2020) to set aside the ex parte order and obtain an extension of time to file their Written Statement of Defence, which remained pending and undecided. Additionally, the third respondent (Najeeb Yeslam Saeed, the alleged purchaser/occupier) was not a party to the main land suit. The High Court (B.S. Masoud, J.) sustained the respondents’ preliminary objection that the injunction application was premature and incompetent: the applicants had no locus to seek affirmative interlocutory relief in a suit where their active standing was barred by an active ex parte order, and they could not join a non-party to the main action. The application was struck out with costs without determining the substantive merits of the injunction.',

