      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that following the prosecution’s amendment of the charge from murder to manslaughter on 24 September 2021, the offence became bailable under Section 148(1) of the Criminal Procedure Act (Cap. 20). In the absence of any evidence indicating that the applicants would abscond, tamper with investigations, or face safety risks, and considering the Republic’s non-objection and the constitutional presumption of innocence, bail was granted subject to strict conditions including two sureties each and travel restrictions.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Ramadhani s/o Issa @ Mohamed and Gerald s/o Paul @ Rutu applied for bail pending trial under Section 148(1) of the Criminal Procedure Act (Cap. 20 R.E. 2019) in Criminal Sessions Case No. 117 of 2020. The applicants were originally arraigned on charges of murder concerning the death of Seleman s/o Rashid on 5 January 2020 at Ngarenanyuki, Arumeru District. On 24 September 2021, the prosecution substituted the murder charge with the lesser, bailable offence of manslaughter under Sections 195 and 198 of the Penal Code (Cap. 16). The applicants filed their bail application with a certificate of urgency, asserting that they were first-time offenders, family breadwinners, and had reliable sureties. State Attorney Alice Mtenga for the Republic stated that the prosecution had no objection to bail. The High Court (M.R. Gwae, J.) confirmed that manslaughter is bailable, noted the lack of any evidence of flight risk, witness interference, or peril to the applicants’ safety, and reaffirmed the constitutional presumption of innocence. Bail was accordingly granted subject to six specific conditions (two sureties each with local authority/employer letters, valid photo IDs, travel restrictions within the jurisdiction without court leave, good conduct, assessment by the Deputy Registrar in the presence of a State Attorney, and execution of bail bonds).',

