      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that while the prosecution established through watertight visual identification (motorcycle headlamp, moonlight, close proximity, and prior familiarity) that the accused struck the deceased with a blunt object causing a fatal skull fracture and intracranial bleeding, the fatal assault arose from a sudden, heated family quarrel without premeditation or arrival with dangerous weapons. Because malice aforethought was not proved beyond reasonable doubt, the accused was acquitted of murder, convicted of manslaughter under Section 195 of the Penal Code, and sentenced to five years’ imprisonment.',
      fullTextSummary: 'David John @ Silwela was arraigned on an indictment for murder under Section 196 of the Penal Code (Cap. 16 R.E. 2019) in Criminal Session No. 1 of 2020 before the High Court at Sumbawanga. The prosecution alleged that on 14 May 2019 at Kasisiwe, following a domestic dispute with his wife (Pendo Albert Simwanza) and a confrontation with his in-laws, the accused ambushed his brothers-in-law (Alex Simwanza and Aidan Simwanza) as they rode a motorcycle to report the disturbance to the police, striking Alex on the head with a blunt object. Alex suffered a fractured skull and intracranial bleeding, dying the following day (15 May 2019). The prosecution called seven witnesses; the defence testified in denial. The High Court (D.B. Ndunguru, J.) found that: (1) The death was unnatural; (2) The nighttime visual identification by Aidan Simwanza was watertight due to intimate familiarity, close-quarters frontal attack, illumination from the motorcycle headlamp and moonlight, and immediate naming; (3) The accused caused the fatal blow; however, (4) Malice aforethought was not proved because the incident was a spontaneous escalation of a domestic fight where the accused arrived without dangerous weapons and lacked premeditation. Consequently, David was acquitted of murder, convicted of manslaughter under Section 195, and sentenced to five years’ imprisonment taking into account 2 years and 7 months spent in remand.',

