      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that under Section 43 of the Employment and Labour Relations Act (Cap. 366) read together with the sanctity of contract, the employer’s obligation to repatriate an employee upon termination is governed by the specific terms of their freely executed agreement. Where Clause 6(c) of the contract expressly permits the employer to provide transport in kind or pay a third-party carrier directly, and specifically stipulates that transportation money will not be paid in cash to the employee, the employee cannot demand direct cash payment. The continuing subsistence allowance was quashed because the employee failed to prove he presented his luggage for transport and the employer refused; however, unpaid 2017 annual leave and undisputed gratuity and salary arrears were upheld.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Maro Msamba, an Assistant Lecturer in Mathematics, Economics, and Commerce at Stella Maris Mtwara University College (STEMMUCO), was employed under renewable three-year contracts from 14 November 2011 until 6 October 2017, when the College declined to renew his contract. The employee accepted non-renewal but claimed terminal benefits (gratuity, salary arrears, 2017 annual leave, repatriation to Mara, and continuing subsistence allowance). The CMA awarded all claims, including direct cash payment for repatriation and daily subsistence until final settlement. The College applied for labour revision before the High Court. The High Court (Z.G. Muruke, J.) held that: (1) Clause 6(c) of the employment contract entitled the employer to arrange transport in kind or pay a carrier directly, expressly prohibiting cash payments to the employee; (2) The employee failed to prove he presented his personal effects for transport or that the employer refused to perform, rendering the continuing subsistence award baseless; (3) The employer failed to adduce proof that 2017 annual leave had been taken or paid, so that award was affirmed; and (4) Undisputed gratuity (TZS 14,111,737) and salary arrears were affirmed subject to deduction of TZS 2,000,000 already paid.',

