      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that where the police receive advance intelligence concerning a suspected offence and have ample time to organize an operational team and travel to the scene, there is no emergency under Section 38(1) of the Criminal Procedure Act (Cap. 20) justifying a warrantless search. Seniority of an officer (Superintendent) and the suspected presence of a firearm do not dispense with the mandatory requirement of obtaining a search warrant or written authorization under Section 38 CPA and Police General Order No. 226. Because the warrantless search constituted a substantial illegality violating constitutional protections of privacy and dignity, it created reasonable doubt, rendering the conviction unsafe.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Athanas Amon appealed to the High Court of Tanzania at Dar es Salaam against his conviction and sentence by the Kibaha District Court in Economic Case No. 07 of 2020, where the prosecution alleged that a Beretta pistol (Registration No. 5A7056ZB) and six rounds of ammunition were recovered from a vehicle during a police intercept at the old weighing bridge at Kibaha. The appellant raised eight argued grounds of appeal challenging the legality of the search conducted without a warrant, absence of independent civilian witnesses, questionable chain of custody, contradictions between prosecution witnesses (PW3 and PW5), and failure to evaluate the defence case. The High Court (L. E. Mgonya, J.) held that: (1) The search was governed by Section 38 of the Criminal Procedure Act and Police General Order No. 226; (2) The police received advance intelligence and had sufficient opportunity to procure a search warrant or written authorization from a magistrate or officer in charge; (3) No genuine emergency existed under Section 38(1) CPA; (4) The senior rank of the conducting officer (Superintendent) and the firearm nature of the case do not excuse non-compliance with the statutory warrant requirement; and (5) The resulting illegality created serious reasonable doubt going to the root of the prosecution’s case. The High Court allowed the appeal, quashed the conviction, set aside the sentence, and ordered the appellant’s immediate release.',

