      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that where an applicant for extension of time raises a serious issue of illegality—specifically whether the employer possesses statutory immunity from legal process under the Diplomatic and Consular Immunities and Privileges Act (Cap. 356)—the CMA errs by dismissing the extension on grounds of inadequate explanation. The tribunal should not prematurely decide or require conclusive proof of the substantive immunity at the extension stage, but should extend time so that the jurisdictional immunity question can be properly examined during the application to set aside the ex-parte award.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Shariff Asham Tarimo, employed as an Assistant Lecturer by the Centre for Foreign Relations from 1 September 2012 until his termination for alleged misconduct in October 2018, instituted Labour Dispute No. CMA/DSM/TEM/716/2018 before the CMA at Temeke. The matter proceeded in the employer’s absence and resulted in an ex-parte award on 12 July 2019 in Tarimo’s favour. Upon becoming aware of the award after the limitation period expired, the Centre applied for an extension of time to apply to set aside the ex-parte award, asserting non-service and diplomatic immunity from suit under the Diplomatic and Consular Immunities and Privileges Act (Cap. 356). The CMA dismissed the extension application for insufficient cause. On revision, the High Court Labour Division (S. M. Maghimbi, J.) held that the claimed diplomatic immunity constituted a serious question of illegality and jurisdiction that warranted substantive consideration rather than summary rejection at the extension threshold. Crucially, the High Court did not decide whether the Centre actually possessed immunity, but held that the issue deserved a full hearing on the merits. The revision was allowed, the CMA ruling was set aside, and the Centre was granted 30 days from 23 December 2022 to file an application before the CMA to set aside the ex-parte award.',

