      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that while Rule 5(6) of the Judicial Review Procedure and Fees Rules 2014 applies strictly after leave to apply for judicial review has been granted, this inapplicability does not deprive the Court of its inherent or procedural power to grant an interim injunction during pending leave proceedings where an applicant establishes a serious question to be investigated and temporary protection is required to preserve the status quo. Order XLIII Rule 2 of the Civil Procedure Code permits applications to be made orally, and the supporting affidavit (paragraph 14) had already disclosed the intention to seek interim restraint.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Chalinze Cement Company Limited filed Miscellaneous Cause No. 591 of 2022 seeking leave to apply for judicial review (orders of certiorari and prohibition) against a December 2022 communiqué issued by the Fair Competition Commission (FCC) concerning a proposed merger involving Scancem International DA and Tanga Cement. The applicant contended that the FCC communiqué contradicted an earlier binding determination of the Fair Competition Tribunal in Consolidated Appeals Nos. 6, 10 and 12 of 2022 which had prohibited the merger. While the leave application was pending, Chalinze Cement orally requested an interim injunction to restrain the FCC from implementing the decision. The FCC opposed the request, arguing that the Attorney General was not summoned under Section 18 of the Law Reform Act, a separate formal written application was required, the chamber summons did not specify the prayer, and Rule 5(6) of the Judicial Review Rules only permits interim relief after leave is granted. The High Court (M. K. Ismail, J.) ruled that: (1) Rule 5(6) applies post-leave, but does not preclude the court from issuing interim restraint at the leave stage; (2) Order XLIII Rule 2 of the Civil Procedure Code permits oral applications; (3) Paragraph 14 of the supporting affidavit gave sufficient notice of the requested interim relief; (4) The applicant demonstrated a serious question requiring judicial investigation; and (5) Substantive issues (reviewability of the communiqué, jurisdiction, merits of the merger) were matters for later determination and must not be prejudged. The High Court granted the interim injunction restraining the FCC from implementing the December 2022 communiqué or effecting the merger pending determination of the leave application.',

