      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that "status quo" means preserving the situation existing at the time the court considers the application, not restoring a party to an anterior position (status quo ante). Because the disputed agricultural land had already been cultivated with maize growing under the respondent or his tenant, ordering the property restored to the applicants would constitute a mandatory dispossession that prematurely determines possession/ownership prior to hearing Land Appeal No. 4 of 2022. Furthermore, courts are strictly bound by pleadings, and the applicants’ chamber summons fatally asked that the respondent continue using the property.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Five applicants (Dalangu Gidabuldgald, Elias Gundanga Derema, Omari Mkasa, Emanuel Gichenoka, and Gumba Suhosu) filed Miscellaneous Land Application No. 05 of 2022 in the High Court of Tanzania at Babati seeking an interlocutory order to preserve/restore their use of disputed agricultural farmland pending determination of Land Appeal No. 4 of 2022. The underlying dispute originated from Land Application No. 25 of 2022 before the Babati District Land and Housing Tribunal, which was dismissed on a technical ground without deciding title. The applicants alleged that while the appeal was pending, the respondent (Dilala Digabulgalda) forcibly repossessed the land and leased it to Ezekiel Marco Dalega, who cultivated maize. The respondent denied the lease, alleged forgery reported to the police, and argued that the current physical status quo (standing maize crop) favoured him. The High Court (G. N. Barthy, J.) dismissed the application on two main grounds: (1) An order for maintenance of status quo preserves the present factual state of affairs at the time of adjudication rather than restoring an antecedent position (status quo ante); ordering repossession would prejudge the substantive ownership appeal; and (2) The applicants’ chamber summons contained a fatal drafting contradiction by formally praying that the respondent continue using the land, and a court cannot grant remedies outside the formal pleadings. The application was dismissed with costs to follow the event.',

