      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that: (1) A previous judgment by a Primary Court or criminal court that did not determine ownership before a competent land tribunal does not operate as res judicata under Section 9 CPC; (2) A building permit merely authorizes construction and does not prove legal title or ownership of land; (3) Hashimu Shabani proved ownership on a balance of probabilities through a purchase agreement from Juma Mohamed and corroborative evidence from neighbouring plot occupiers confirming Gilbert encroached beyond his purchased one-room portion; and (4) An appellate court will not disturb trial tribunal findings of fact absent misdirection or disregard of material evidence.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Daniel Gilbert appealed to the High Court of Tanzania at Morogoro (M. J. Chaba, J.) against the judgment of the Morogoro District Land and Housing Tribunal in Land Application No. 202 of 2016, which declared Hashimu Shabani the lawful owner of a disputed parcel of unsurveyed land. Gilbert raised five grounds of appeal (arguing grounds 1 & 3, and 4 & 5 together, having abandoned ground 2), contending that the dispute was barred by res judicata under Section 9 CPC pursuant to Morogoro Urban Primary Court Civil Case No. 216 of 1999, and that the Tribunal erred by finding for Shabani despite Gilbert holding a building permit and site plan. The High Court dismissed the appeal on all grounds: (1) The doctrine of res judicata did not apply because prior criminal appeals (Nos. 30/2015 and 08/2016) and High Court Land Appeal No. 170 of 2019 established that the ownership of the disputed property had never been conclusively resolved by a competent land adjudication forum; (2) Under Section 110 of the Evidence Act, the standard of proof in civil cases is on a balance of probabilities (Hemedi Saidi v Mohamed Mbilu [1984] TLR 113); (3) Shabani proved his ownership through a sale agreement from Juma Mohamed and corroborative testimony of long-standing neighbours (Tarsisi Mawala and Ahmad Iddi Itigilo) showing Gilbert bought only an adjoining one-room parcel and subsequently encroached onto Shabani’s land; (4) Gilbert failed to produce any sale agreement from his alleged vendor (Stamili Rajabu), and a building permit authorizes construction but does not constitute proof of legal ownership; and (5) No misdirection was established to warrant appellate interference. The appeal was dismissed with costs.',

