      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that Regulation 34(1) of the Employment and Labour Relations (General) Regulations (G.N. No. 47 of 2017) is cast in mandatory terms ("shall"), requiring any party seeking revision of a CMA award to first lodge the prescribed notice of intention to seek revision using CMA Form No. 10. Where the applicant offers no genuine explanation for the total omission, the overriding-objective principle cannot be deployed to bypass a mandatory statutory requirement, rendering the revision application incurably incompetent and liable to be struck out.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Flomi Hotel Limited applied to the High Court of Tanzania, Labour Division at Morogoro (M. J. Chaba, J.) in Labour Revision Case No. 01 of 2022 to revise an award of the Commission for Mediation and Arbitration (CMA) delivered on 10 December 2021 by Arbitrator Kayugwa in Labour Dispute No. CMA/MOR/185/2020. The respondents (Emmanuel Sylvester Manga and Warren G. Mbwambo) raised a point of preliminary objection asserting that the revision was fatally defective and incompetent because Flomi Hotel failed to lodge CMA Form No. 10 (Notice of Intention to Seek Revision) at the CMA as mandatorily required by Regulation 34(1) of the Employment and Labour Relations (General) Regulations, G.N. No. 47 of 2017. The applicant conceded that it had not filed CMA Form No. 10, but argued (relying on Tanzania Revenue Authority v Mulamuzi Byabusha) that the form was a mere procedural step that did not institute the High Court revision, that a Notice of Application had already been filed under the Labour Court Rules without causing prejudice, and that the overriding objective justified allowing an amendment. The High Court upheld the preliminary objection and struck out the revision: (1) Regulation 34(1) uses the mandatory term "shall", making the filing of CMA Form No. 10 an obligatory prerequisite; (2) The applicant failed to provide any genuine explanation for its non-compliance; (3) The overriding-objective principle cannot be used to sanction a total disregard of clear, mandatory statutory provisions; and (4) Non-compliance renders the revision incurably incompetent. The revision was struck out without delving into the substantive merits of the CMA award, with each party bearing its own costs.',

