      relevantPassage: 'The High Court held that: (1) An identification parade conducted in breach of Rule 2(s) of Police General Order No. 232—by omitting to record the mandatory question regarding the connection in which the suspect is identified and the witness’s reply—is given no evidential weight; (2) Nevertheless, the surviving husband’s visual recognition of Jacob Enock Shindika was reliable and watertight due to prior acquaintance, 4 hours of interaction, and bright solar lighting; (3) The cautioned statements of the second, third, and fourth accused were voluntary and corroborated by eyewitness testimony; (4) The first accused’s bus ticket alibi was disproved as a fabrication via TRA records linking the ticket registration to a motorcycle; and (5) Under Section 23 of the Penal Code (common intention) and Section 200 (malice aforethought), all four accused were guilty of murder committed during an armed gold robbery and were sentenced to death by hanging.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Four accused persons (Jacob Enock Shindika, Boazi Mudi Saidi, Rashidi Ramadhani Mwaulezi, and Japhet Boniphace Sanga) stood trial before the High Court of Tanzania at Mbeya (R. A. Ebrahim, J.) in Criminal Sessions Case No. 7 of 2020 for the murder of Mwasi Mwangembe at Itumbi Hamlet, Matundasi Village, Chunya District, contrary to Section 196 of the Penal Code. On 15 August 2018, the accused visited the home and gold-buying office of the deceased’s husband, Luheta Nhandi Limbu, pretending to bring gold-bearing sand for processing. After remaining on the brightly lit premises for approximately four hours, they launched a coordinated armed attack using machetes, heavy metal objects, a knife, and a firearm. They tied up Limbu, severely beat the occupants, struck Mwasi on the head with a machete, pierced the victims’ eyes, and stole 260 grams of gold and money. Mwasi succumbed to traumatic skull fractures on 17 September 2018. The High Court rejected the formal identification parade evidence because the parade registers failed to record the mandatory question and answers required by Rule 2(s) of PGO No. 232. However, the Court convicted all four accused based on: (1) Watertight visual recognition of Jacob by Limbu under bright solar lighting over a 4-hour period; (2) Voluntary cautioned statements of Boazi, Rashidi, and Japhet admitting their roles and detailing the conspiracy, corroborated by Limbu’s testimony; (3) Disproved alibis, notably Jacob’s bus ticket containing a vehicle registration that TRA records proved belonged to a motorcycle owned by WU ZHOU Investment Co. Ltd; and (4) The doctrine of common intention (Section 23 Penal Code) and inferred malice aforethought (Section 200 Penal Code) from lethal weapons targeted at vital organs. All four accused were convicted of murder and sentenced to death by hanging.',

