      relevantPassage: 'The High Court found the neighbour’s visual identification unreliable because his claim of directing a torch at armed killers from seven metres for 15 minutes while fearing for his life was unrealistic. Furthermore, disputed and retracted confessions cannot corroborate one another as corroboration must stem from independent evidence. With the identification rejected and no independent corroboration remaining, the charge was not proved beyond reasonable doubt and all three accused were acquitted.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Majuto Ngamba alias Ntumbi, Edward Maganga alias Tungu, and Paschal January alias Malosha were jointly charged with murdering Keflin daughter of Masanja at Segese Village, Kahama District. The prosecution alleged that three attackers invaded the deceased’s home at night, inflicting fatal cut wounds that caused death by haemorrhagic shock. The prosecution relied on the alleged nighttime visual identification of Edward Maganga by neighbour Abel William using a torch, and cautioned and extra-judicial statements recorded before police and a Ward Executive Officer. The High Court (S. M. Kulita, J.) rejected the eyewitness identification as inherently unrealistic and unsafe. The Court also held that disputed confessions cannot corroborate each other, as corroboration must come from independent evidence. With no reliable independent evidence remaining, the prosecution failed to prove the case beyond reasonable doubt, and all three accused were acquitted and ordered to be released immediately.',

