      relevantPassage: 'An appeal lodged on the 60th day of a 60-day court-granted extension is strictly within time. Furthermore, under section 19 of the Law of Limitation Act, the time requisite for obtaining certified copies of the judgment and proceedings is excluded. The omission of a date beside the appellant’s signature does not invalidate a Memorandum of Appeal where the court registry officially endorsed and stamped the date of filing upon receipt; the appellant may be permitted to insert that date.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Shaban Juma Kisonga (as legal representative of the estate of the late Pili Selemani Kiula) appealed against a land decision of the Iramba District Land and Housing Tribunal in favour of Jumanne Omary (representing the estate of the late Omary Mjange Dudu). The respondent raised two preliminary objections: (1) that the appeal was time-barred beyond the 60-day extension period granted on 28 March 2022 in Misc. Land Application No. 38 of 2020; and (2) that the appeal was incompetent because the Memorandum of Appeal was undated. The High Court (George M. Masaju, J.) overruled both objections: (1) The 60-day period expiring from 28 March 2022 culminated on 27 May 2022, which was the exact date the appeal was filed; additionally, Section 19 of the Law of Limitation Act excludes time required to obtain copies of judgment and proceedings. (2) The Memorandum was signed and officially stamped/endorsed by the Tribunal registry on 27 May 2022, so the omission of a handwritten date did not render the appeal incompetent. The appellant was permitted to insert the date 27 May 2022, both objections were overruled with each party bearing own costs, and the substantive land appeal was declared competent to proceed.',

