      relevantPassage: 'A court may issue an interim status quo order to preserve property connected to pending litigation where continued extra-judicial seizure or sale could undermine the proceedings and the restrained parties have not demonstrated that temporary preservation would cause them injustice. The addition of a recovery agent not party to the main suit does not disconnect the application where the agent acts under the instructions of the principal bank regarding the same debt and secured property.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Vigu Trading Company Limited obtained credit facilities totaling approximately USD 2.8 million from Bank of Africa Tanzania Limited between 2013 and 2016, secured by ~100 commercial trucks, truck heads and trailers. When disputes arose over debt balances, interest calculations, and recovery fees exceeding TZS 140 million, Vigu instituted Civil Case No. 147 of 2020, which was referred to court-supervised mediation. During the mediation, the bank appointed recovery agents (Nampula Auction Mart and Bilo Star Debt Collector) who impounded or threatened to impound and auction 27 and 18 additional vehicles without transparent debt accounting. Vigu applied in Misc. Civil Application No. 560 of 2022 for an interim order maintaining the status quo. The respondents opposed, arguing that the 3rd respondent was not party to the main suit, that a previous application (Misc. Civil Application No. 547 of 2020) was dismissed for want of prosecution, and that the application lacked urgency. The High Court (H. R. Mwanga, J.) granted the interim status quo order, holding that: (1) The application remained inextricably connected to Civil Case No. 147 of 2020 because both arose from the same banking facilities and secured property; (2) Agents acting on bank instructions did not sever the jurisdictional nexus; (3) Status quo preservation was necessary to prevent disruption of business, protect the subject matter, and safeguard the integrity of pending judicial mediation; and (4) The respondents failed to demonstrate any injustice. All three respondents were ordered to stop impounding, seizing or selling the scheduled vehicles pending determination of the application on its merits.',

