      relevantPassage: 'A stolen television is sufficiently identified through a purchase receipt, detailed descriptions of size and brand, recovery records, and uncontested ownership, even where it is not physically shown to the complainant during trial. Accomplice testimony implicating an accused who led police to the scene and demonstrated the break-in is corroborative. However, merely being named by a co-accused during police interrogation without an admitted cautioned statement or independent evidence cannot sustain a conviction.',
      fullTextSummary: 'Andrea Theophil and Emanuel Paskal alias Ema Kibaka appealed to the High Court of Tanzania at Arusha (M. R. Gwae, J.) in Criminal Appeal No. 42 of 2023 against their convictions by the District Court of Karatu for housebreaking (Section 294(1) Penal Code) and stealing (Sections 258 & 265 Penal Code). The prosecution proved that the house of Fatuma Juma was broken into and a 32-inch Hisense television, smartwatch, and cash stolen. Police recovered the television from co-accused Antony Lenga, who testified that Andrea sold it to him. Andrea then led police to the complainant’s house and demonstrated how the break-in occurred. The High Court affirmed Andrea’s convictions: (1) The television was sufficiently identified via the purchase receipt and recovery records under Nyerere Nyague v Republic, even though not physically displayed to the complainant in court; (2) Antony’s accomplice testimony was corroborated by Andrea’s demonstration at the crime scene and failure to cross-examine on material facts; (3) The trial court’s procedural failure to invite comments on exhibit admissibility did not prejudice Andrea. Conversely, the High Court allowed Emanuel’s appeal because the sole allegation against him was that Andrea mentioned his name during police interrogation, which was uncorroborated, Andrea’s cautioned statement was not tendered, and the State conceded the evidence was insufficient. Emanuel was ordered to be released immediately.',

