      // 2. Executive Summary
      executiveSummary: 'Ibrahim Mohamed appealed against his conviction for an unnatural offence involving alleged non-consensual anal penetration. The complainant stated that she initially agreed to have ordinary sexual intercourse with the appellant for TZS 10,000 at Pilson Guest House. She alleged that, when she attempted to leave, the appellant caused her to fall and forcibly penetrated her anus. She raised an alarm, and other people allegedly came to the room. Although medical evidence showed bruising and blood around the complainant’s anus, the High Court found serious weaknesses in the prosecution’s case. The complainant’s account raised credibility questions, there was an unexplained two-day delay before the medical examination, and crucial witnesses who allegedly witnessed the incident or responded to the alarm were not called. The Court held that the charge was not proved beyond reasonable doubt. It allowed the appeal, quashed the conviction, set aside the 30-year sentence and ordered the appellant’s release.',

      summary: {
        background: 'The appellant was convicted of an unnatural offence under Section 154(1)(a) of the Penal Code and sentenced to 30 years’ imprisonment by the District Court of Chunya in Criminal Case No. 45 of 2022. He appealed to the High Court challenging the credibility of the complainant, an unexplained two-day delay in medical examination, failure to call crucial eyewitnesses, and failure to prove guilt beyond reasonable doubt.',
        legalIssue: 'Whether the prosecution proved the charge of unnatural offence beyond reasonable doubt where the victim’s testimony had internal credibility flaws, the medical PF3 was delayed by two days without explanation, and the key Good Samaritan eyewitness was not called.',
        reasoning: 'The High Court held that while a sexual offence conviction can legally rest on uncorroborated victim testimony (Section 127(6) Evidence Act; Selemani Makumba; Edward Nzabuga), that testimony must be reliable, truthful, and credible (Mohamed Said). Here, the complainant’s story had unexplained contradictions regarding the agreed duration and transition to non-consensual acts. Furthermore, an unexplained 2-day delay in obtaining the PF3 examination weakened the link between the injuries and the alleged event. Finally, the unexplained failure to call the Good Samaritan eyewitness who allegedly caught the appellant in the act justified an adverse inference against the prosecution (Aziz Abdallah; Pascal Sele).',
        finalDecision: 'Appeal allowed; conviction quashed; 30-year imprisonment sentence set aside; appellant ordered released immediately.',
        legalPrinciples: [
          'A sexual-offence conviction may be based on the victim’s evidence without corroboration, but only where that evidence is credible, truthful and reliable.',
          'Where the victim\'s account contains significant credibility problems, medical examination is inexplicably delayed, and an available material eyewitness is not called, the resulting doubt must be resolved in favour of the accused.'
        ]
      },

