      // 6. Court Reasoning
      courtReasoning: [
        'Burden and Standard of Proof: The Court applied section 110 of the Evidence Act: a person who makes an allegation must prove it. In criminal cases, the prosecution carries the burden beyond reasonable doubt (Pascal Yoya @ Maganga v Republic), which never shifts to the accused.',
        'Admitted Preliminary Facts: Facts admitted during the preliminary hearing (the commercial sex agreement, raising an alarm, being at Pilson Guest House) required no proof. The appellant’s subsequent denial during defence was an afterthought, but the State was still obligated to prove the offence.',
        'Victim Evidence in Sexual Offences: A conviction may be founded on the uncorroborated evidence of a sexual offence victim under Section 127(6) of the Evidence Act (Selemani Makumba v Republic; Edward Nzabuga v Republic), but such evidence must pass strict tests of truthfulness, reliability, and credibility (Mohamed Said v Republic).',
        'Credibility Concerns: The complainant claimed the agreement was for the entire night, yet testified she dressed and tried to leave immediately after the first act, and that the appellant waited until she had dressed before forcing anal intercourse. These unanswered questions rendered her account untrustworthy.',
        'Unexplained Medical Delay: The alleged incident occurred on 28 March 2022, but the PF3 examination was conducted on 30 March 2022. The 2-day unexplained delay created reasonable doubt as to whether the observed bruising and bleeding were caused by the appellant or an intervening event.',
        'Failure to Call Material Eyewitnesses: Although Section 143 of the Evidence Act does not require a specific number of witnesses (Yohanes Msigwa v Republic), quality and materiality govern. The Good Samaritan who allegedly caught the appellant in the act was a material eyewitness. Unexplained failure to call him entitled the court to draw an adverse inference against the prosecution (Aziz Abdallah v Republic; Pascal Sele v Republic; Omari Hussein alias Ludanga v Republic).'
      ],

