        appellantArguments: 'Mr. Majaliwa Wiga argued that: (1) Evidence of a sexual offence victim must not be accepted wholesale and must pass strict truthfulness, credibility, and reliability tests; (2) It is highly unusual for a victim subjected to force to remain with the assailant for ~3 hours and accompany him to her residence without explaining why she did not flee or call for help; (3) PW1 and PW2 gave contradictory evidence regarding the mother’s arrival time (11:00 p.m. vs 1:00 a.m.); (4) The prosecution failed to call crucial witnesses, namely the landlord who received the first complaint and the arresting officer; (5) These fatal gaps created reasonable doubt requiring the conviction and sentence to be quashed.',
        respondentArguments: 'Mr. Sadik Abdulkheri, State Attorney, argued that: (1) The prosecution proved rape beyond reasonable doubt; (2) The complainant knew Alfa beforehand as he called her by name; (3) Penetration and absence of consent were established; (4) The medical practitioner’s evidence and PF3 corroborated the victim’s account; (5) PW4 (police investigator) corroborated key elements; (6) Time differences between PW1 and PW2 were minor discrepancies and failure to call the landlord was non-fatal.'
