      summary: 'Diana Makiluli filed a divorce petition against her husband, Enock Rabieth. The parties informed the District Court that their marriage had irretrievably broken down and signed a deed settling custody, child maintenance, access and matrimonial property. After the hearing had concluded but before judgment was delivered, Enock allegedly died. The District Court subsequently delivered judgment on 15 August 2024, dissolved the marriage and adopted the parties’ settlement. Diana appealed, arguing that the marriage had automatically ended upon Enock’s death and that the divorce case should have abated. She maintained that the District Court had been informed about the death before delivering judgment. The High Court found no electronic court record proving that the District Court had been informed. More importantly, it held that the Civil Procedure Code could fill the procedural gap in the Law of Marriage Act. Under Order XXII Rule 6, a case does not abate when a party dies after the hearing has concluded but before judgment is pronounced. Because the parties had already been heard and only judgment remained, the District Court was legally permitted to deliver its decision. The appeal was dismissed without costs.',
