      fileName: 'Neema Gabriel Majaliwa vs Saraweki Israel Salema and 3 Others (Civil Appeal No 442 of 2022) 2024 TZCA 1308 (27 December 2024).pdf',
      summary: 'The dispute concerned the intestate estate of Rashidi Mangana Marwa, who died in November 2017. Following an initial annulled appointment in the Primary Court, appellant Neema Gabriel Majaliwa petitioned the District Delegate’s Court at Dodoma in Probate Case No. 24 of 2019 and obtained letters of administration on 19 June 2019. On revision, the High Court annulled her letters of administration because the petition was not accompanied by mandatory consents from all heirs or required explanatory affidavits under Rule 39 of the Probate and Administration Rules (GN No. 163 of 1963), and recognized first respondent Saraweki Israel Salema as a lawful beneficiary. The Court of Appeal (Lila, Muruke, Mdemu, JJ.A.) dismissed the appeal, holding that Rule 39 is mandatory and requires all consents and explanatory affidavits to accompany the petition at the time of lodging; later filings cannot retroactively cure an incompetent petition. However, the Court held that once the High Court found the petition incompetent, it erred in determining substantive beneficiary status for the first respondent, which must be decided de novo in a future, properly instituted administration proceeding. Costs in all three courts were awarded to the respondents.',
