      legalIssues: [
        'Whether the appellant’s petition for letters of administration was incompetent from inception due to failure to attach mandatory heirs’ consents and explanatory affidavits required under Rule 39 of the Probate and Administration Rules.',
        'Whether late submission of heirs’ consents and explanatory affidavits can cure a petition for letters of administration that was defective at the time of lodging.',
        'Whether the High Court, after declaring the administration petition incompetent and void, had legal competence to determine substantive beneficiary/widow status for the first respondent.',
        'Whether disputed customary marriage and beneficiary entitlement must be resolved in a fresh, competent probate proceeding.'
      ],
      partiesArguments: {
        appellantArguments: 'Neema Gabriel Majaliwa argued that: (1) Her petition for letters of administration was properly filed and the High Court erred in annulling her appointment; (2) The first respondent never proved a valid customary marriage with the deceased under the Law of Marriage Act; (3) When she married the deceased, she was not informed of any pre-existing customary marriage; (4) She recognized the sons (2nd, 3rd, and 4th respondents) and had distributed their portions without dispute; (5) The record erroneously recorded that she recognized the first respondent as a lawful widow.',
        respondentArguments: 'Ms. Tecla Adolph Kimati argued that: (1) Rule 39(f) of the Probate and Administration Rules mandatorily requires heirs’ consents to accompany the petition when filed; (2) Jordan Rashidi Mangana was a minor and could not consent without a legal guardian under Rule 71(3); (3) Robert Mangana’s consent and explanatory affidavits under Rule 72(1) were filed months after the petition; (4) The District Delegate should have stayed proceedings and treated the matter as a suit upon caveat under Section 58(3) and Rule 82; (5) First respondent’s customary marriage was recognized by the family meeting and Section 55(g) Law of Marriage Act; (6) Appeal should be dismissed with costs.'
      },
      courtReasoning: [
        'MANDATORY NATURE OF RULE 39 PROBATE RULES: Rule 39 of the Probate and Administration Rules (GN No. 163 of 1963) prescribes mandatory accompaniments for an administration petition. Where consent of all heirs is not obtained, Rule 39(f) read with Rules 71 and 72 requires explanatory affidavits to be filed together with the petition at the exact moment of lodging.',
        'INCOMPETENCE AB INITIO CANNOT BE CURED RETROACTIVELY: The petition was filed on 20 March 2019 without consents or explanatory affidavits. Robert’s consent was lodged on 18 June 2019, Jordan was an unrepresented minor, and known heirs were omitted without contemporaneous explanation. The petition was defective from inception; subsequent filing of documents cannot validate an incompetent petition (relying on Rashidi Hassan v. Mrisho Juma).',
        'PROPER DISPOSITION OF INCOMPETENT PROCEEDINGS: Once a court determines that an originating petition is incompetent, the entire proceeding is void. A court cannot proceed to decide substantive rights, heirship, or validity of marriages (such as Saraweki’s customary marriage) in an incompetent proceeding. Preliminary legal issues that terminate a suit must be decided first without venturing into substantive merits.',
        'FRESH PETITION REQUIRED: The status of the first respondent and substantive distribution must be determined de novo if raised in a future, properly instituted probate proceeding.'
      ],
