      fileName: 'Penzia Nicodemus Haule vs Vertex Group Experts Limited 2024 TZHC 11169 (27 December 2024).pdf',
      summary: 'Penzia Nicodemus Haule applied under Section 68(e) and Order XXXVII Rule 1(1) of the Civil Procedure Code for a temporary injunction restraining Vertex Group Experts Limited from entering or remaining on Plot No. 77, Block N, Makambako, where she operated a community dispensary, pending determination of main Land Case No. 23735 of 2024. She claimed the property was matrimonial property acquired with her late husband Thobias Mbilinyi and could not be mortgaged without spousal consent. The respondent showed the property belonged to Nazareti Company Ltd / Nazareth Medical Centre Ltd, was mortgaged to Grofin SGB Ltd / Grofin AGB Ltd to secure a loan that defaulted, and was sold to the respondent as a bona fide purchaser. The High Court (D.B. Ndunguru, J.) dismissed the application with costs, applying Atilio v. Mbowe and holding that: (1) Marriage alone does not prove property is matrimonial property or establish a prima facie case of ownership; (2) Generalized claims of dispensary disruption do not establish irreparable loss that cannot be compensated by damages (Tanzania Cotton Marketing Board v. Cogecat Cotton); and (3) The balance of convenience does not favour restraining a bona fide purchaser at the instance of a defaulting borrower’s claimant.',
