      partiesArguments: {
        applicantArguments: 'Mr. Nictagon Itege argued that: (1) Catherine Kashonda was neither the biological nor legally adopted daughter of the deceased; (2) The respondents knew this but falsely listed her as a daughter and beneficiary, misleading the court; (3) Family recognition alone does not legally create inheritance rights; (4) Exhibit PJK-3 was unsigned, unstamped, of unknown authorship, and unverified; (5) Misrepresentation of heir identity constitutes sufficient ground to revoke the grant under Section 49; (6) Fresh letters of administration should be granted to the applicant.',
        respondentArguments: 'Mr. Yiga Joseph argued that: (1) Catherine was raised and educated by the deceased from childhood, married under his sponsorship, and publicly bore the family name; (2) The family consistently recognized and treated her as a daughter; (3) Elfredina Balilemwa’s affidavit was from an in-law and failed to establish fraud; (4) Fraud must be strictly proved to a high standard under Section 110/117 of the Evidence Act; (5) The applicant attended the family meeting, signed Form No. 56 consenting to the appointment, and filed no caveat after citation; (6) The applicant is estopped from alleging concealment of openly disclosed facts; (7) Administrator suitability is distinct from final distribution; (8) Application should be dismissed with costs.'
      },
      courtReasoning: [
        'HEIGHTENED BURDEN OF PROVING FRAUD: A person alleging fraud under Section 49(1)(b) and (c) carries the burden of proving it with cogent, compelling evidence. Revocation is a serious remedy, and allegations of fraudulent wrongdoing require stronger proof than ordinary civil averments. Showing that Catherine might not be a biological daughter does not prove that the administrators knowingly made a false statement or deliberately concealed material facts.',
        'OPEN DISCLOSURE NEGATES CONCEALMENT: Catherine’s identity and status were openly discussed during the family meeting, accepted by the family, and expressly listed in the probate petition and Form No. 56. Statutory citation was published without objection or caveat.',
        'ESTOPPEL AND CLEAN HANDS: The applicant participated in the family nomination, signed Form No. 56, and raised no contemporaneous objection. Having consented to the appointment with full knowledge of Catherine’s listing, the doctrine of estoppel applies against him, and he failed to approach the court with clean hands.',
        'APPOINTMENT VERSUS FINAL INHERITANCE: The court distinguished the appointment of suitable administrators from the final determination of lawful heirs. At the appointment stage, the inquiry is whether administrators are fit and proper. Whether Catherine is ultimately entitled to share in estate distribution may be raised and adjudicated during subsequent administration, inventory confirmation, or estate distribution proceedings.',
        'CONTINUING STATUTORY SUPERVISION: The administrators remain bound by oath under Section 66 to administer faithfully and must file an amended inventory and accounts under Section 107 subject to court supervision.'
      ],
