      ratioDecidendi: 'While penetration in a sexual offence prosecution may be established by the credible testimony of the victim without medical corroboration, the testimony must be coherent, consistent, and free from unresolved material gaps. A medical finding that a minor complainant is not a virgin does not establish that recent penetration occurred or identify the accused as the perpetrator. Where the prosecution fails without explanation to call a material witness who could clarify central factual conflicts, the court may draw an adverse inference. To sustain a conviction for abduction under Section 134 of the Penal Code, the prosecution must strictly prove an unlawful taking from lawful custody against the will of the parent or guardian; voluntary or open accompaniment among community members does not suffice.',
