        applicantArguments: 'Advocate Sosthenes Mselingwa argued that: (1) The applicant diligently pursued an appeal before the Court of Appeal which was struck out on 18 March 2025 for technical record defects; (2) Time spent prosecuting the earlier appeal is recognized as technical delay and good cause (Fortunatus Masha v William Shija); (3) The fresh application was filed promptly within 15 days on 2 April 2025; (4) Denying an extension would unfairly deprive him of his constitutional right to be heard (Article 13(6)(a) Constitution, Mobrama Gold Corp v Minister for Energy); (5) The application should be granted in the interests of justice.',
        firstAndSecondRespondentsArguments: 'Advocate Walter Massawe for CRDB Bank PLC and City Land Company Ltd argued that: (1) The applicant explained the period before the Court of Appeal ruling but failed to account for the days after 18 March 2025; (2) An applicant must strictly account for every material day of delay; (3) Extension of time is discretionary and cannot be granted where post-ruling delay is unexplained; (4) The application should be dismissed with costs.',
        thirdRespondentArguments: 'Advocate Margreth Mbasha for Abdallah Abubakar Saggaf argued that: (1) The chamber summons only prayed for extension of time to file a notice of appeal and omitted a prayer for serving the letter requesting proceedings under Rule 84(1); (2) Parties are bound by their pleadings (Zebra International Enterprises v Antony Mrisho Ngai); (3) The delay was not adequately explained; (4) The application should be dismissed with costs.'
