      ratioDecidendi: 'Time spent by a litigant diligently prosecuting an appeal that is ultimately struck out for technical or procedural anomalies in the record of appeal constitutes technical delay and sufficient cause for granting an extension of time under Section 11(1) of the Appellate Jurisdiction Act and Section 14(1) of the Law of Limitation Act. The principle requiring an applicant to account for every day of delay is a prudential standard assessing whether the entire substantial period is reasonably explained, not a rigid mathematical accounting of each calendar day. A 15-day interval between the striking out of an appeal and the filing of an extension application is not inordinate and confirms ongoing diligence. An order extending time to file a notice of appeal properly covers both the filing and service of the notice and the service of the letter requesting copies of proceedings under Rule 84(1) of the Court of Appeal Rules.',
