      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, DAR ES SALAAM DISTRICT REGISTRY
MISC. CIVIL APPLICATION NO. 548 OF 2018 / [2020] TZHC 10146
(Arising from Civil Appeal No. 40 of 2017)

ASHIRA K. NURAD .................................................... APPLICANT
VERSUS
SAID RAMADHANI DINYA .............................................. RESPONDENT

RULING
S.M. KULITA, J. (Delivered on 31 December 2020):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Ashira K. Nurad applied for an extension of time to seek restoration of Civil Appeal No. 40 of 2017, which had been dismissed for want of prosecution on 14 June 2018.

She explained that her former advocate, Yassin Membar, suffered from heart disease, was admitted to the Jakaya Kikwete Cardiac Institute and later died. Because he was a sole practitioner, she experienced difficulties learning about the dismissal and obtaining her case file from his office.

The High Court accepted that the advocate’s sickness and death were circumstances outside the applicant’s control. It found sufficient cause, granted an extension of time and directed that restoration be undertaken within 30 days. No costs were awarded.

2. MATERIAL FACTS (Pages 1–2 and 4):
- Civil Appeal No. 40 of 2017 was dismissed for want of prosecution on 14 June 2018.
- The applicant had been represented by Advocate Yassin Membar.
- The advocate suffered from heart disease and was admitted to the Jakaya Kikwete Cardiac Institute for approximately three months.
- He died on 20 March 2018.
- He operated as a sole practitioner.
- His beneficiaries took time to notify his clients and locate the applicant’s case file.
- The applicant stated that she did not know that her appeal had been dismissed until she followed up with the court.
- She consequently failed to apply for restoration within the required time.

3. LEGAL ISSUES & ARGUMENTS (Pages 2–4):
Main Issue: Whether the applicant established sufficient cause under Section 14(1) of the Law of Limitation Act to extend time for restoring the dismissed appeal.
Applicant: Advocate hospitalization, demise on 20 March 2018, and sole practitioner hurdles beyond her control. Represented by Legal and Human Rights Centre (LHRC).
Respondent: Alleged lack of genuine reason, negligence, 463 days delay without day-to-day accounting, and absence of illegality allegations (citing Mathew Martin v Kahama Mining and PS Min of Defence v Devram Valambhia).

4. REASONING & OPERATIVE ORDERS (Pages 4–5):
Sickness and death of an advocate are circumstances outside human control. In those circumstances the applicant was effectively helpless. The explanation amounted to sufficient cause.
Extension of time and restoration should ordinarily be pursued together when relying on the same facts.
FINAL ORDERS:
1. Extension of time granted.
2. The applicant is required to restore Civil Appeal No. 40 of 2017 within 30 days from 31 December 2020.
3. No order as to costs because the underlying dispute involved a family matter.`,
