      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, LABOUR DIVISION AT DAR ES SALAAM
MISC. LABOUR APPLICATION NO. 436 OF 2019 / [2020] TZHCLD 3844

EZEKIAH TOM OLUOCH .................................................... APPLICANT
VERSUS
CHAMA CHA WALIMU TANZANIA ............................................ RESPONDENT

RULING
I.D. ABOUD, J. (Delivered on 31 December 2020):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Ezekiah Tom Oluoch filed an urgent labour application against Chama Cha Walimu Tanzania. He challenged his suspension and dismissal as Deputy General Secretary, disputed a by-election held on 4 June 2018 and asked the court to declare him the respondent’s General Secretary. His claims depended principally on the interpretation of Article 43(1) and Article 23(2)(g) of the respondent’s Constitution and Rule 31(b) of its internal rules. The documents attached by the applicant did not contain those essential provisions (the attached Constitution ended at Article 31.3, while Rule 31(b) was also unavailable). The court could not interpret provisions that were not before it and could not find them from other sources. The court therefore held that the application was incompetent and struck it out.

2. MATERIAL FACTS (Pages 1–4):
- The applicant had been Deputy General Secretary of Chama Cha Walimu Tanzania.
- The respondent reportedly suspended and later dismissed him.
- The General Secretary, Yahya B.K. Msulwa, died on 17 November 2017.
- The respondent conducted a by-election in Dodoma on 4 June 2018.
- Christopher Banda and Deus G. Seif were elected Vice President and General Secretary respectively.
- The applicant challenged the jurisdiction and supervision of that election.
- He claimed that the respondent should have applied its Constitution and internal rules to fill the vacancy.

3. LEGAL ISSUES & REASONING (Pages 4–6):
- A party asking a court to interpret an institution’s Constitution or internal rules must place the relevant provisions before the court.
- The attached version of the Constitution ended at Article 31.3, so Article 43(1) was unavailable. Rule 31(b) was also missing.
- Courts cannot determine claims based on missing or unavailable legal instruments.
- The person making an allegation must prove it.
- No decided cases were expressly cited in the ruling.

4. FINAL ORDERS (Page 6):
1. The application is found incompetent.
2. The application is struck out from the court registry.
3. Substantive declarations not determined on merits.
4. No separate order as to costs.`,
