      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, DAR ES SALAAM DISTRICT REGISTRY
LAND CASE NO. 34 OF 2014 / [2020] TZHC 10070

GOLDEN REEF LTD ....................................................... PLAINTIFF
VERSUS
ULF NILSSON ........................................................... DEFENDANT

JUDGMENT
S.M. KULITA, J. (Delivered on 31 December 2020):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Golden Reef Ltd sued Ulf Nilsson, claiming that he wrongfully possessed Certificate of Title No. 45952 for Plot No. 15, Block “A”, Kimbiji. The plaintiff sought return of the certificate, damages, interest and costs. The dispute depended on who lawfully controlled Golden Reef Ltd. Captain Lazarus Abel Sanga and Meinrad Florian Haule claimed that Lloyd Marthinsen and Otto Arne Holte had transferred the company’s shares to them in 2008. Marthinsen and Holte denied transferring their shares and alleged that the transfer documents were forged, maintaining that they had entrusted the title deed to Ulf Nilsson for safekeeping before leaving Tanzania. The High Court found that the alleged share transfer was not proved (passports showed Marthinsen and Holte were abroad on the signing date). Ulf Nilsson’s possession of the title was therefore lawful, and the plaintiff’s suit was dismissed with costs against Sanga and Haule.

2. MATERIAL FACTS (Pages 1–5):
- Golden Reef Ltd was the registered owner of Certificate of Title No. 45952 for Plot No. 15, Block “A”, Kimbiji.
- Captain Sanga and Meinrad Haule claimed to have acquired company shares on 14 November 2008.
- Transfer documents were filed with BRELA on 10 June 2012 (four years later).
- Marthinsen and Holte denied transferring shares; passport stamps proved they were outside Tanzania on 14 November 2008.
- Marthinsen and Holte gave company documents and title deed to Ulf Nilsson for safekeeping before returning to Norway.
- No written custody agreement was produced; Nilsson admitted possessing the title on their instructions.

3. LEGAL ISSUES & REASONING (Pages 5–9):
- Personal familiarity with signatures (Advocate Ngalo) is insufficient to prove authenticity where forgery is in issue without handwriting-expert evidence.
- 4-year delay before BRELA filing created serious doubts.
- Passport stamps conclusively showed alleged transferors were out of the country.
- An oral entrustment for title safekeeping is lawful.
- No statutory provisions or judicial precedents were expressly cited in the judgment.

4. FINAL ORDERS (Page 10):
1. Title was lawfully entrusted to defendant Ulf Nilsson.
2. Plaintiff not entitled to repossess Certificate of Title No. 45952.
3. Plaintiff’s suit dismissed.
4. Costs ordered against Captain Lazarus Abel Sanga and Meinrad Florian Haule.`,
