      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, DAR ES SALAAM DISTRICT REGISTRY
CIVIL CASE NO. 195 OF 2015 / [2020] TZHC 10000

HASS PETROLEUM (T) LIMITED ............................................. PLAINTIFF
VERSUS
NJAKE ENTERPRISES AND OIL TRANSPORT LIMITED ........................... DEFENDANT

RULING
Y.J. MLYAMBINA, J. (Delivered on 23 December 2020):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
During the trial of a commercial dispute, the plaintiff’s witness, Hamis Diriye Abdi, attempted to tender invoices, delivery notes, release orders, transaction lists and loading documents. The defendant objected on two main grounds: first, that the invoices were computer-generated documents that did not satisfy section 18 of the Electronic Transactions Act; second, that several documents were photocopies or secondary evidence violating section 66 of the Tanzania Evidence Act.

The High Court held that merely entering information into a computer and printing an invoice does not necessarily make the invoice a computer-generated electronic record. The electronic-evidence objection was therefore rejected. The court also found that copies acknowledged by the defendant in its Written Statement of Defence and list of documents were admissible under section 67(1)(b) of the Evidence Act. However, ten documents that had not been pleaded were excluded. The objections were consequently partly sustained.

2. MATERIAL FACTS (Pages 1–4):
- The main proceedings were Civil Case No. 195 of 2015.
- PW1, Hamis Diriye Abdi, sought to tender numerous commercial documents (invoices, delivery notes, release orders, loading tickets, transaction records) covering transactions occurring mainly between 2011 and 2013.
- The defendant characterized the invoices as computer-generated printouts requiring Section 18 certification.
- The defendant also argued that some documents were copies rather than originals.
- Some copied documents had been acknowledged in paragraph 4(iii) of the defendant’s Written Statement of Defence and list of documents.
- Ten additional documents had not been pleaded.

3. LEGAL ISSUES & REASONING (Pages 6–9):
- Electronic evidence: The court distinguished between a document merely typed or prepared using a computer and a record generated, stored, or transacted through an electronic system. Merely inputting information and printing an invoice does not make it a computer-generated record requiring electronic certification.
- Secondary evidence: Acknowledged copies are admissible under Section 67(1)(b) of the Tanzania Evidence Act.
- Unpleaded documents: Ten unpleaded documents were excluded because parties are bound by their pleadings and no proper explanation was provided.

4. LAWS CITED:
- Electronic Transactions Act, 2015 (Section 3, Section 18, Section 18(2))
- Tanzania Evidence Act, 1967 (Section 66, Section 67(1)(b))
- Indian Evidence Act, 1872 (Section 65)

5. CASES CITED:
- Paschal John Macha v Access Bank Tanzania Ltd and Another, Civil Application No. 151 of 2013
- Serengeti Breweries Ltd v Breakpoint Outdoor Caterers Ltd, Commercial Case No. 132 of 2014
- Gopalkrishnan @ Dileep v State of Kerala and Another, Criminal Appeal No. 1794 of 2019 (India)
- Jagmail Singh and Others v Karamjit Singh and Others, Civil Appeal No. 1889 of 2020 (India)
- Ramadhani Salum Kinyogo v Tanroads and Two Others, Land Case No. 18 of 2012

6. FINAL ORDERS:
1. Objection against computer-printed invoices rejected.
2. Objection against acknowledged copies rejected.
3. Objection against ten unpleaded documents upheld.
4. Evidentiary objections partly sustained.
5. Costs to follow the event. Main civil suit remains pending.`,
