      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, MBEYA DISTRICT REGISTRY
MISC. LAND APPLICATION NO. 45 OF 2018 / [2020] TZHC 4342
(Arising from the decision of the District Land and Housing Tribunal for Mbeya in Land Application No. 153 of 2010)

LEONARD KAWANA (LEONARD KAWAWA) ........................................ APPLICANT
VERSUS
1. DEUS ANDREW
2. 2ND RESPONDENT
3. 3RD RESPONDENT
4. 4TH RESPONDENT ..................................................... RESPONDENTS

RULING
D.B. NDUNGURU, J. (Delivered on 23 December 2020):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Leonard Kawana applied for more time to appeal against the decision of the District Land and Housing Tribunal for Mbeya in Land Application No. 153 of 2010. The High Court found that he had continuously attempted to pursue his appeal. Part of the delay resulted from waiting for court records, while another part arose after an earlier appeal was struck out for being improperly drafted. The Court treated this as a technical delay, rather than negligence, and granted him 45 days to file the intended appeal.

2. MATERIAL FACTS (Pages 1–4):
- The District Land and Housing Tribunal delivered its decision on 23 September 2015.
- The proceedings and judgment became available on 6 May 2016, approximately seven months later.
- On 10 June 2016, the applicant filed an application for extension of time.
- That application was granted.
- On 9 March 2017, he filed Land Appeal No. 19 of 2017.
- The appeal was later struck out because its grounds were considered argumentative.
- He filed the present extension application on 6 July 2018.
- The second, third and fourth respondents did not appear or file counter-affidavits.

3. LEGAL ISSUES & REASONING (Pages 4–8):
- Good/Sufficient Cause: The court explained that granting an extension of time is discretionary and depends upon the circumstances of each case.
- Continuous Pursuit: Although an applicant is normally required to account for every day of delay, the Court found that Kawana had been continuously attempting to pursue his appeal, describing him as being in the “court corridor tirelessly pursuing” the matter.
- Technical Delay: The earlier proceedings and the defective appeal constituted a technical delay. The Court found no deliberate inaction or negligence and concluded that the respondents would not suffer material prejudice.

4. LAWS CITED:
- Section 41(2) of the Land Disputes Courts Act, Cap. 216 R.E. 2002
- The Written Laws (Miscellaneous Amendments) Act No. 2 of 2016

5. CASES CITED:
- FINCA (T) Ltd & Another v Boniface Mwalukisa
- Kalunga Company Advocates Ltd v National Bank of Commerce Ltd (2006) TLR 235
- Attorney General v Mkongo Building and Civil Works Contractors Ltd & Another
- Samwel Munsiro v Chacha Mwikwabe
- Selina Chibago v Finihas Chibago
- Bushiri Hussein v Latifa Lukio Mashayo
- Emmanuel R. Maira v District Executive Director, Bunda District Council
- Victor Rweyemamu Binamungu v Geofrey Kabaka & Another

6. FINAL ORDERS:
1. The application was found to have merit.
2. The applicant was granted 45 days from 23 December 2020 to file the intended appeal.
3. There was no order as to costs.`,
