      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA AT MOSHI (LAND DIVISION)
LAND CASE NO. 08 OF 2018 / [2020] TZHC 4626

1. MARTINA MARTIN SILAYO
2. EMILIA MARTIN LYAKITURU (AMEN MARTIN SHAO) .......................... PLAINTIFFS
VERSUS
1. GODFREY MARTIN SILAYO
2. MARTIN & SONS COMPANY LIMITED & 11 OTHERS .......................... DEFENDANTS

JUDGMENT
S.B. MKAPA, J. (Delivered on 23 December 2020):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
The plaintiffs, widows of the late Martin Alex Silayo, claimed ownership rights over commercial property at Plot No. 6, Block I, Section III, Market Street, Moshi Municipality (Certificate of Title No. 056041/54). The deceased had transferred the property to Martin & Sons Company Limited in 2013 for one shilling without obtaining spousal consent. The High Court held that the property was acquired during the deceased’s polygamous marriage and constituted matrimonial property. Its transfer without spousal consent violated Section 59(1) of the Law of Marriage Act and was declared null and void. The property remains subject to pending probate proceedings.

2. MATERIAL FACTS (Pages 1–6):
- The deceased was married to three wives in a polygamous marriage.
- Disputed property: Plot No. 6, Block I, Section III, Market Street, Moshi (Title No. 056041/54).
- Initially registered solely in the deceased’s name.
- In 2013, the deceased transferred it to Martin & Sons Company Limited for a nominal consideration of one shilling.
- The plaintiffs did not consent to the transfer.
- The second plaintiff had previously signed a spousal consent form when the property was used as collateral for a CRDB Bank loan.
- The defendants claimed the property was commercial and never occupied by the wives.
- A probate case concerning the deceased’s estate remained pending.

3. LEGAL ISSUES & REASONING (Pages 6–11):
- Scope of Matrimonial Property: Matrimonial property is broader than the matrimonial home. Commercial property acquired during marriage is matrimonial property even if spouses never resided in it.
- Lack of Spousal Consent: Transfer to the company without spousal consent contravened Section 59(1) of the Law of Marriage Act and was void.
- Registration in One Spouse's Name: Individual registration does not extinguish equitable spousal interests.
- Limitation of Land Suit: Monetary and motor vehicle claims dismissed as they belong to probate administration.

4. LAWS CITED:
- Section 110(1) of the Evidence Act (Cap. 6)
- Section 59(1) of the Law of Marriage Act (Cap. 29 R.E. 2019)
- Section 2 of the Law of Marriage Act
- Section 112(2) of the Land Act (Cap. 113)

5. CASES CITED:
- Abdul-Karim Haji v Raymond Nchimbi Alois and Joseph Sita Joseph (2006) TLR 420
- Afred Kinunda v Maria Kumburu, Matrimonial Appeal No. 2 of 2019, [2020] TZHC 1673
- National Bank of Commerce Ltd v Nurbano Abdallah Mulla, Civil Appeal No. 283 of 2017, [2020] TZCA 238
- Gabriel Nimrod Kurwijila v Theresia Hassani Malongo, Civil Appeal No. 102 of 2018

6. FINAL ORDERS (Pages 11–12):
1. Transfer to Martin & Sons Company Limited declared null and void.
2. Property declared matrimonial property of the deceased and his three wives.
3. Property remains subject to pending probate proceedings.
4. Remaining claims (mesne profits, damages, bank funds, motor vehicle) dismissed.
5. Each party to bear own costs.`,
