      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, ARUSHA DISTRICT REGISTRY
CIVIL REVISION NO. 5 OF 2020 / [2020] TZHC 4447
(Arising from Civil Revision No. 06 of 2018 of the District Court of Arusha)

1. STEPHEN SAMWEL KIMBELE
2. CHRISTOPHER SAMWEL KIMBELE .......................................... APPLICANTS
VERSUS
1. NEMBRIS SAMWEL KIMBELE
2. RUTH SAMWEL KIMBELE
3. NAANYUNI SAMWEL KIMBELE ............................................. RESPONDENTS

RULING
M.R. GWAE, J. (Delivered on 23 December 2020):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
The applicants challenged Civil Revision No. 06 of 2018, which had been handled by the District Court of Arusha after originating from Primary Court proceedings. They argued that the District Court revision was filed outside the statutory period of 12 months and that the District Court therefore lacked jurisdiction. Counsel for the respondents accepted that the revision was time-barred. The High Court found that it had been determined approximately 22 months and 12 days after the relevant Primary Court decision. Because the statutory time limit had expired, the District Court lacked jurisdiction. Its proceedings, ruling and drawn order were consequently quashed and set aside.

2. MATERIAL FACTS (Pages 1–3):
- The dispute originally came from proceedings in a Primary Court.
- The respondents commenced Civil Revision No. 06 of 2018 before the District Court of Arusha.
- District Court revisions involving Primary Court proceedings had to be commenced within 12 months.
- The matter was entertained approximately 22 months and 12 days after the relevant Primary Court determination.
- The applicants challenged the legality of those proceedings before the High Court.
- The respondents’ advocate, Mr Moses Mahuna, conceded that the District Court revision was outside the prescribed period.

3. LEGAL ISSUES & REASONING (Pages 2–3):
- Fundamental Jurisdiction: Jurisdiction is fundamental and must be established before a court considers the merits.
- Creature of Statute: A court’s jurisdiction is created and limited by legislation. Parties cannot confer jurisdiction by consent.
- Statutory Limitation: Section 22(4) of the Magistrates’ Courts Act limits the District Court’s revisional jurisdiction over Primary Court proceedings to 12 months.
- Nullity: Because approximately 22 months and 12 days had passed, the District Court acted outside the statutory period and lacked jurisdiction.

4. LAWS CITED:
- Sections 30(1)(a), 30(1)(b)(i), 31(1), 31(2) and 32(2) of the Magistrates’ Courts Act, Cap. 11 R.E. 2002
- Section 22(4) of the Magistrates’ Courts Act, Cap. 11 R.E. 2019

5. CASES CITED:
- Shyam Thanki and Others v New Palace Hotel [1971] 1 EA 199
- Fanuel Mantiri Ng’unda v Herman Mantiri Ng’unda & 20 Others, Civil Appeal No. 8 of 1995

6. FINAL ORDERS (Page 3):
1. The District Court lacked jurisdiction to entertain Civil Revision No. 06 of 2018.
2. The District Court’s proceedings were quashed.
3. Its ruling was quashed and set aside.
4. Its drawn order was quashed and set aside.
5. No separate order concerning costs.`,
