      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, MBEYA DISTRICT REGISTRY
DC CRIMINAL APPEAL NO. 26 OF 2021 / [2021] TZHC 9267
(Arising from Criminal Case No. 91 of 2013 of the District Court of Mbeya)

1. EMMANUEL MARTIN
2. JOHN WAZIRI ......................................................... APPELLANTS
VERSUS
REPUBLIC ............................................................... RESPONDENT

JUDGMENT
J.M. KARAYEMAHA, J. (Delivered on 29 November 2021):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Emmanuel Martin and John Waziri appealed against their convictions and 30-year prison sentences for armed robbery. The High Court found that the trial magistrate merely summarized the evidence but failed to analyse and evaluate the prosecution and defence evidence. The trial judgment therefore failed to comply with Section 312 of the Criminal Procedure Act. The appeal was allowed without determining whether the appellants committed the offence. The case was returned to the trial court for a magistrate with competent jurisdiction to recompose a proper judgment after evaluating all the evidence.

2. MATERIAL FACTS (Pages 1–4):
- Alleged robbery occurred on 5 May 2013 at Songwe Prison in Mbeya.
- A prison officer, Ambrose Ngonyani, collected a SAR firearm for guard duty and was allegedly attacked and robbed at his residence.
- Emmanuel Martin was arrested and police claimed the firearm was recovered from John Waziri's house on 29 May 2013.
- Both appellants denied involvement in the offence.

3. LEGAL ISSUES & REASONING (Pages 6–13):
- Failure to Evaluate Evidence: The trial magistrate only narrated witness testimony without analysing credibility, weight, or comparing prosecution evidence to the defence.
- Section 312 CPA: A criminal judgment must contain reasoned findings on points for determination.
- Role of First Appellate Court: Although an appellate court can re-evaluate evidence, questions of witness demeanor and credibility require evaluation by the trial court.
- Incurable Defect: The total absence of evidence evaluation requires recomposition of judgment.

4. LAWS CITED:
- Section 287A of the Penal Code, Cap. 16
- Section 240(3) of the Criminal Procedure Act, Cap. 20
- Section 312(1) of the Criminal Procedure Act, Cap. 20
- Section 10A of the Written Laws (Miscellaneous Amendments) Act No. 3 of 2011

5. CASES CITED:
- Posolo Wilson @ Mwalyengo v Republic, Criminal Appeal No. 613 of 2013
- Rashid Roman Nyerere v Republic, Criminal Appeal No. 105 of 2014
- Michael Mgowole & Another v Republic, Criminal Appeal No. 205 of 2017
- Kifaru Juma Kifaru & Others v Republic, Criminal Appeal No. 126 of 2018
- Prince Charles Junior v Republic, Criminal Appeal No. 250 of 2014
- Shija Masawe v Republic, Criminal Appeal No. 158 of 2007
- Republic v Alphonce Jackson, Criminal Appeal No. 87 of 2019

6. FINAL ORDERS (Pages 13–14):
1. Appeal allowed on procedural grounds.
2. Conviction and 30-year sentences set aside pending recomposition.
3. Case returned to trial court for a magistrate of competent jurisdiction to recompose judgment complying with Section 312 CPA.
4. Appellants to be brought before trial court when summoned.
5. Right of appeal against recomposed judgment preserved.
6. Not an order for a new trial.`,
