      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, MBEYA DISTRICT REGISTRY
PROBATE APPEAL NO. 2 OF 2020 / [2021] TZHC 9463
(Arising from Revision Application No. 3 of 2020 of the District Court)

JULIETH JACKSON SEVERINE & KASHAIJA JACKSON SEVERINE .................... APPELLANTS
VERSUS
FREDRICK KAIZA BIFANIMANYA (Administrator of Estate of Jackson Severine) . RESPONDENT

JUDGMENT
A.A. MBAGWA, J. (Delivered on 31 December 2021):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
The appellants challenged the appointment of Fredrick Kaiza Bifanimanya as administrator of the estate of the late Jackson Severine. Their initial appeal to the District Court was struck out because they had not been parties to the original Primary Court probate case. They were advised to seek revision instead. They submitted a written complaint asking the District Court to exercise revisional powers under Section 22 of the Magistrates’ Courts Act. The District Court struck out the request because the complaint did not cite Section 22. The High Court quashed this decision, holding that Section 22 does not prescribe one mandatory format for initiating revision (it can be via formal application, complaint letter, oral representation, or suo motu), and complaint letters need not cite enabling statutory provisions. The appeal was allowed, and the revision was ordered to be heard on its merits.

2. MATERIAL FACTS (Pages 1–3):
- Fredrick Kaiza Bifanimanya was appointed administrator of Jackson Severine’s estate in Probate Cause No. 77 of 2019 at Uyole Primary Court.
- The appellants challenged the appointment in Civil Appeal No. 14 of 2019, which was struck out because they were non-parties.
- Advised that revision was the proper remedy, they submitted written complaint letters requesting District Court revision.
- The respondent raised a preliminary objection under Order XLIII Rule 2 CPC.
- The District Court ruled CPC inapplicable, yet struck out the revision for failure to cite Section 22 MCA.
- Respondent served via substituted service but did not appear; appeal heard ex parte.

3. LEGAL ISSUES & REASONING (Pages 4–8):
- Section 22 MCA Revisional Format: Does not prescribe a single mandatory procedure. Revisions can be triggered by formal summons, written complaint letter, oral statement, or suo motu.
- Statutory Citation Rule: Formal court pleadings require enabling citations; informal complaint letters by citizens do not.
- Natural Justice Violation: District Court raised and decided the non-citation point on its own without hearing the parties.
- CPC Inapplicability: Order XLIII Rule 2 CPC does not govern revisions originating from Primary Courts.

4. LAWS CITED:
- Section 22(1) of the Magistrates’ Courts Act, Cap. 11
- Section 20(3) of the Magistrates’ Courts Act, Cap. 11
- Section 2 of the Civil Procedure Code, Cap. 33
- Order XLIII Rule 2 of the Civil Procedure Code, Cap. 33
- Section 53(2) of the Interpretation of Laws Act, Cap. 1

5. CASES CITED:
- None cited in judgment (decided on statutory interpretation).

6. FINAL ORDERS (Page 8):
1. Appeal allowed.
2. District Court ruling and striking out order quashed and set aside.
3. Revision Application No. 3 of 2020 ordered to be heard on its merits.
4. Right of further appeal retained.`,
