      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, SUMBAWANGA DISTRICT REGISTRY
CRIMINAL SESSION NO. 1 OF 2020 / [2021] TZHC 9116

THE REPUBLIC ........................................................... PROSECUTOR
VERSUS
DAVID JOHN @ SILWELA ................................................... ACCUSED

JUDGMENT
D.B. NDUNGURU, J. (Delivered on 31 December 2021):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
David John @ Silwela was charged with murdering his brother-in-law, Alex Simwanza. The prosecution alleged that David attacked Alex and Aidan Simwanza while they were riding a motorcycle to report an earlier family disturbance to the police. Aidan identified David using the motorcycle headlamp and moonlight. The Court found the identification reliable and concluded that David caused Alex’s fatal head injury. However, the death followed a family quarrel and physical fight. The Court found no evidence that David had planned or intended to kill Alex. Malice aforethought was therefore not proved. David was acquitted of murder but convicted of the lesser offence of manslaughter and sentenced to five years’ imprisonment.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- David John @ Silwela was married to Pendo Albert Simwanza.
- Alex Simwanza (deceased) was David’s brother-in-law.
- On 14 May 2019, David and his wife had a domestic dispute, and Pendo went to Kasisiwe.
- David followed her on a bicycle; confrontation ensued with in-laws.
- Later that evening, Alex and Aidan were riding a motorcycle to the police when they were attacked near sisal plants.
- Aidan identified David through motorcycle headlamp and moonlight.
- Alex suffered a skull fracture and intracranial bleeding, dying on 15 May 2019.
- David arrested on 15 May 2019; admitted conflict with in-laws but denied fatal assault at trial.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
1. Whether Alex Simwanza died an unnatural death.
2. Whether David John @ Silwela caused the fatal injuries.
3. Whether nighttime identification by Aidan was reliable.
4. Whether David acted with malice aforethought.
5. Whether established facts proved murder or manslaughter.
6. What sentence was appropriate.

4. LAWS CITED:
- Penal Code, Cap. 16 R.E. 2019 (Sections 195, 196, 197, 198)

5. FINAL DECISION AND ORDERS:
1. Accused found not guilty of murder.
2. Accused found guilty and convicted of manslaughter under Section 195 of the Penal Code.
3. Accused sentenced to five (5) years’ imprisonment under Section 198 of the Penal Code.

SOURCE WARNINGS:
- Date discrepancy: Passage states family informed of death on 5 May 2019 (actual death 15 May 2019).
- Typo: Reference to 15 May 2011 instead of 15 May 2019.
- Exhibit numbering: Post-mortem report cited interchangeably as Exhibit P2 and Exhibit P1.`,
