      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, MTWARA DISTRICT REGISTRY
LABOUR REVISION NO. 01 OF 2021 / [2021] TZHC 9126
(Arising from Labour Dispute No. CMA/MTW/LD/143/17 of the CMA at Mtwara)

STELLA MARIS MTWARA UNIVERSITY COLLEGE (STEMMUCO) ....................... APPLICANT
VERSUS
MARO MSAMBA ........................................................... RESPONDENT

JUDGMENT
Z.G. MURUKE, J. (Delivered on 30 December 2021):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Maro Msamba worked for Stella Maris Mtwara University College under renewable three-year employment contracts. The College decided not to renew his second contract when it expired on 6 October 2017. The employee claimed gratuity, salary arrears, annual leave, repatriation expenses and subsistence allowance. The CMA awarded several of those claims. The High Court held that the employment contract allowed the College to provide transport or pay a transport company directly. It did not require the College to give the employee cash for transportation. The employee also failed to prove that he presented his luggage for transportation according to the contract and that the College refused to transport it. The subsistence award was therefore quashed. However, the College failed to prove that the employee had received his 2017 annual leave entitlement. That award was upheld, together with the undisputed gratuity and salary arrears.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- Maro Msamba employed as assistant lecturer starting 14 November 2011.
- Completed two 3-year contracts; second term ended 6 October 2017.
- College gave non-renewal notice on 22 August 2017.
- Employee accepted non-renewal and lodged terminal claims of TZS 28,961,737.
- College admitted gratuity and salary arrears, paying TZS 2,000,000 on account.
- Dispute arose over direct cash repatriation vs Clause 6(c) transport in kind and daily subsistence.
- CMA awarded cash repatriation and continuing subsistence.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Whether employee entitled to cash repatriation payment from Mtwara to Mara.
- Whether entitled to continuing subsistence allowance.
- Whether entitled to 2017 annual leave.
- Whether express terms of Clause 6(c) bound both parties.

4. LAWS CITED:
- Employment and Labour Relations Act, Cap. 366 R.E. 2019 (Section 43)
- Law of Contract Act, Cap. 345 R.E. 2019 (Section 19(1), Section 39)

5. FINAL DECISION AND ORDERS:
1. Subsistence allowance from 6 October 2017 until full payment quashed and set aside.
2. Repatriation to be executed pursuant to Clause 6(c) of employment contract.
3. 2017 annual leave payment affirmed.
4. Gratuity of TZS 14,111,737 and salary arrears of TZS 2,896,625 affirmed, less TZS 2,000,000 paid.
5. Decretal sums payable within three (3) months from 1 January 2022.
6. Each party to bear own costs.

SOURCE WARNINGS:
- Conflicting salary-arrears figures: Judgment body states TZS 2,896,825 while final signed decree states TZS 2,896,625.`,
