      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, LABOUR DIVISION AT DAR ES SALAAM
REVISION NO. 296 OF 2022 / [2022] TZHCLD 1121
(Arising from Labour Dispute No. CMA/DSM/TEM/716/2018 of the CMA at Temeke)

BOARD OF DIRECTORS, CENTRE FOR FOREIGN RELATIONS ....................... APPLICANT
VERSUS
SHARIFF ASHAM TARIMO ................................................... RESPONDENT

RULING
S. M. MAGHIMBI, J. (Delivered on 23 December 2022):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Shariff Asham Tarimo was employed by the Centre for Foreign Relations as an Assistant Lecturer. His employment was terminated in October 2018 for alleged misconduct. He challenged the termination before the Commission for Mediation and Arbitration. The dispute proceeded in the employer’s absence, and an ex-parte award was issued in Tarimo’s favour. After learning about the award, the Centre applied for an extension of time to file an application to set it aside, arguing non-service and immunity under the Diplomatic and Consular Immunities and Privileges Act (Cap. 356). The CMA rejected the extension. On revision, the High Court held that the claimed diplomatic immunity raised a serious issue of illegality deserving proper examination and should not have been rejected prematurely. The High Court did not decide whether the Centre possessed immunity, only that the issue deserved a hearing.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- Shariff Asham Tarimo employed as Assistant Lecturer starting 1 September 2012.
- Terminated in October 2018 for alleged misconduct.
- Referred dispute to CMA Temeke (No. CMA/DSM/TEM/716/2018); ex-parte award issued on 12 July 2019.
- Centre applied for extension of time to set aside ex-parte award based on non-service and Cap. 356 immunity.
- CMA dismissed extension; Centre applied for revision.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Did CMA err by refusing to extend time to challenge ex-parte award?
- Did claimed diplomatic immunity constitute a serious illegality justifying extension of time?
- Should the immunity question have been finally decided during extension proceedings?

4. LAWS CITED:
- Employment and Labour Relations Act, Cap. 366 (Sections 91(1)(b), 94(1)(b)(i))
- Labour Court Rules, GN 106/2007 (Rules 24, 28)
- Diplomatic and Consular Immunities and Privileges Act, Cap. 356 (Third Schedule Item 29, Fourth Schedule)

5. FINAL DECISION AND ORDERS:
1. Revision allowed.
2. CMA ruling dismissing extension of time set aside.
3. Applicant granted 30-day extension from 23 December 2022 to file application to set aside ex-parte award before CMA.`,
