      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, MOROGORO SUB-REGISTRY
LAND APPEAL NO. 01 OF 2022 / [2022] TZHC 15509
(Arising from Land Application No. 202 of 2016 of the District Land and Housing Tribunal of Morogoro / Shauri No. 01 of 2016 Mwembesongo Ward Tribunal)

DANIEL GILBERT ........................................................... APPELLANT
VERSUS
HASHIMU SHABANI .......................................................... RESPONDENT

JUDGMENT
M. J. CHABA, J. (Delivered on 30 November 2022):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Daniel Gilbert appealed against the decision of the Morogoro District Land and Housing Tribunal in Land Application No. 202 of 2016 declaring Hashimu Shabani the lawful owner of disputed unsurveyed land. Gilbert argued that the suit was barred by res judicata under Section 9 CPC following Civil Case No. 216 of 1999 and that the Tribunal disregarded his building permit and site plan. The High Court dismissed the appeal, holding that: (1) Res judicata did not apply because previous proceedings had not determined ownership in a competent land tribunal; (2) A building permit authorizes construction but does not establish legal ownership; (3) Shabani proved his purchase from Juma Mohamed on a balance of probabilities under Section 110 Evidence Act, supported by neighbouring occupiers confirming Gilbert encroached onto Shabani’s parcel; and (4) The trial tribunal committed no misdirection.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- Dispute over unsurveyed land began in ~1999 (Civil Case No. 216 of 1999).
- Subsequent criminal appeals directed parties to land adjudication tribunal.
- Mwembesongo Ward Tribunal (Shauri No. 01 of 2016) ruled for Shabani.
- District Land and Housing Tribunal (Land Application No. 202 of 2016) declared Shabani lawful owner on 15 December 2021.
- Shabani proved purchase from Juma Mohamed (20x10 steps) and produced neighbour witnesses (Tarsisi Mawala, Ahmad Iddi Itigilo).
- Gilbert claimed purchase from Stamili Rajabu but produced only a building permit and site plan without a sale agreement.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Was the land application barred by res judicata under Section 9 CPC?
- Which party proved ownership on a balance of probabilities under Section 110 Evidence Act?
- Does a building permit establish legal ownership of land?
- Did the trial tribunal properly evaluate the evidence?

4. LAWS CITED:
- Civil Procedure Code, Cap. 33 (Section 9 — Res Judicata)
- Evidence Act, Cap. 6 (Section 110 — Burden of Proof)
- Land Disputes Courts Act, Cap. 216 (Section 3)
- Land Act, Cap. 113 (Section 167)
- Village Land Act, Cap. 114 (Section 62)

5. FINAL DECISION AND ORDERS:
1. Decision of Morogoro District Land and Housing Tribunal upheld.
2. Hashimu Shabani confirmed as lawful owner of the disputed land.
3. Appeal dismissed with costs.`,
