      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, LABOUR DIVISION, MOROGORO DISTRICT REGISTRY
LABOUR REVISION CASE NO. 01 OF 2022 / [2022] TZHC 15706
(Arising from Labour Dispute No. CMA/MOR/185/2020 of the CMA at Morogoro)

FLOMI HOTEL LIMITED ..................................................... APPLICANT
VERSUS
1. EMMANUEL SYLVESTER MANGA
2. WARREN G. MBWAMBO .................................................... RESPONDENTS

RULING
M. J. CHABA, J. (Delivered on 30 December 2022):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Flomi Hotel Limited applied to revise a CMA award delivered on 10 December 2021 by Arbitrator Kayugwa in Labour Dispute No. CMA/MOR/185/2020. The respondents raised a preliminary objection that Flomi Hotel failed to lodge CMA Form No. 10 (Notice of Intention to Seek Revision) at the CMA as required by Regulation 34(1) of G.N. No. 47 of 2017. The High Court upheld the preliminary objection and struck out the revision, holding that Regulation 34(1) is mandatory ("shall") and cannot be bypassed via the overriding objective where no genuine explanation is given.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- CMA Morogoro decided Labour Dispute No. CMA/MOR/185/2020 on 10 December 2021.
- Applicant filed Labour Revision Case No. 01 of 2022 in the High Court.
- Applicant did not file CMA Form No. 10 at the CMA.
- Respondents filed preliminary objection on 18 March 2022.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Is filing CMA Form No. 10 under Regulation 34(1) mandatory?
- Does unexplained failure to file CMA Form No. 10 render the revision incurably incompetent?
- Can the overriding-objective principle save the revision from being struck out?

4. LAWS CITED:
- Employment and Labour Relations (General) Regulations, G.N. No. 47 of 2017 (Regulation 34(1); CMA Form No. 10)
- Employment and Labour Relations Act, Cap. 366 (Sections 91, 94)
- Interpretation of Laws Act, Cap. 1 (Section 53(2))
- Written Laws (Miscellaneous Amendments) (No. 3) Act, 2017 (Act No. 8 of 2017)

5. FINAL DECISION AND ORDERS:
1. Preliminary objection upheld.
2. Labour Revision Case No. 01 of 2022 struck out for procedural incompetence.
3. Each party to bear own costs.`,
