      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, ARUSHA DISTRICT REGISTRY
CRIMINAL APPEAL NO. 116 OF 2022 / [2023] TZHC 23673
(Arising from Criminal Case No. 10 of 2022 of the Resident Magistrate’s Court of Manyara at Babati)

THE DIRECTOR OF PUBLIC PROSECUTIONS ................................................. APPELLANT
VERSUS
1. ABDUL MAJID
2. SELEMON WOLDAY
3. ADINO WOLRORO
4. ABRAHMAN MALAKI
5. DAVID KABEMO
6. DARABA DULLAH
7. MAHAMUSH DEMEKA
8. SALUMU LAMBROS
9. MANRASA ABIYA
10. BUGALA FIKIRE
11. MADAMU ZALAKA
12. MISGANO TAMASKE
13. DAMAKA TAMASKEL .............................................................. RESPONDENTS

IN THE MATTER OF:
ADAM FRANCIS KILAWE (CLAIMANT / INTERESTED PERSON)
CONCERNING MOTOR VEHICLE TOYOTA LAND CRUISER REG. NO. 352 BYY

REVISIONAL ORDER
M. R. GWAE, J. (Delivered on 21 December 2023):

1. SUMMARY OF PROCEEDINGS & REVISIONAL JURISDICTION:
Thirteen foreign nationals were convicted on plea of guilty for unlawful entry into Tanzania under section 45(1)(i) of the Immigration Act in Criminal Case No. 10 of 2022 by the Resident Magistrate's Court of Manyara at Babati, each sentenced to 30 days imprisonment or a fine of TZS 500,000. In forfeiture proceedings concerning a Toyota Land Cruiser Reg. No. 352 BYY allegedly used in the offence, Adam Francis Kilawe wrote a letter dated 13 May 2022 claiming an interest. The trial court ordered the vehicle returned to Kilawe upon proof of ownership without a formal application. The DPP appealed (Criminal Appeal No. 116 of 2022) without joining Kilawe or serving the 13 respondents. Invoking Section 373(1) of the Criminal Procedure Act, the High Court set aside the release order and gave Kilawe 30 days to file a formal application under Section 16(5) of the Proceeds of Crime Act.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- 13 foreign nationals convicted of unlawful entry; prosecution sought forfeiture of Toyota Land Cruiser Reg. 352 BYY.
- Claimant Adam Francis Kilawe submitted informal letter dated 13 May 2022; trial court ordered vehicle released to him upon proof of ownership.
- DPP appealed without joining Kilawe as party and without proving service on the 13 respondents.

3. LEGAL FINDINGS & RATIO DECIDENDI:
- An appellate court cannot decide an appeal challenging a property release order without formally joining the beneficiary and serving respondents.
- Third-party claims opposing asset forfeiture cannot be entertained via informal letters; claimants must follow formal statutory application procedures under Section 16(5) and (6) of the Proceeds of Crime Act.
- Revisional jurisdiction under Section 373(1) CPA enables the High Court to correct serious lower court procedural errors and remit matters for lawful hearing.

4. FINAL REVISIONAL ORDERS:
1. Resident Magistrate’s Court vehicle-release order dated 13 June 2022 is REVISED and SET ASIDE.
2. Adam Francis Kilawe is granted permission under Section 16(5) of the Proceeds of Crime Act to file a formal application within 30 days from 21 December 2023 opposing forfeiture before the Resident Magistrate's Court of Manyara.
3. DPP afforded opportunity to formally reply and be heard on merits.
4. Ownership and forfeiture questions reserved for determination by trial court.`,
