      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, MBEYA SUB-REGISTRY
CRIMINAL APPEAL NO. 108 OF 2023 / [2023] TZHC 23740
(Arising from Criminal Case No. 45 of 2022 of the District Court of Chunya)

IBRAHIM MOHAMED ................................................................. APPELLANT
VERSUS
THE REPUBLIC ....................................................................... RESPONDENT

JUDGMENT
D.B. NDUNGURU, J. (Delivered on 29 December 2023):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Ibrahim Mohamed appealed against his conviction and 30-year sentence for an unnatural offence under Section 154(1)(a) of the Penal Code [Cap. 16 R.E. 2019] at Pilson Guest House, Kasanga Village, Chunya District. The complainant (A.K.) alleged that after agreed commercial sex, the appellant forcibly penetrated her anus. Although medical evidence showed anal bruising and blood, the High Court identified fatal prosecution weaknesses: credibility doubts regarding the transition to forced intercourse, an unexplained two-day delay in medical examination (28 to 30 March 2022), and failure to call the key Good Samaritan eyewitness who responded to the alarm. The conviction and sentence were quashed, and immediate release ordered.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- Alleged incident on 28 March 2022 at Pilson Guest House, Kasanga Village, Chunya District.
- Complainant A.K. agreed to ordinary sexual intercourse with appellant for TZS 10,000.
- Complainant alleged appellant prevented her from leaving, forcibly penetrated her anus causing bleeding and pain.
- Good Samaritan allegedly responded to alarm and caught appellant at scene.
- Medical examination (PF3) performed on 30 March 2022 (unexplained 2-day delay).
- District Court convicted appellant and sentenced him to 30 years’ imprisonment.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Whether complainant\'s evidence was credible and trustworthy (No).
- Whether unexplained 2-day delay in medical examination created reasonable doubt (Yes).
- Whether failure to call the material Good Samaritan eyewitness weakened the State\'s case (Yes, adverse inference drawn).
- Whether the charge was proved beyond reasonable doubt (No).

4. LAWS CITED:
- Penal Code [Cap. 16 R.E. 2019], Section 154(1)(a)
- Evidence Act [Cap. 6 R.E. 2022], Sections 110, 127(6), 143

5. FINAL DECISION AND ORDERS:
1. Appeal ALLOWED.
2. Conviction for unnatural offence QUASHED.
3. Sentence of 30 years’ imprisonment SET ASIDE.
4. Appellant Ibrahim Mohamed ordered to be RELEASED IMMEDIATELY unless lawfully detained for another cause.`,
