      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, MANYARA SUB-REGISTRY AT BABATI
MISC. CIVIL APPLICATION NO. 39 OF 2023 / [2023] TZHC 23484
(Arising from Misc. Civil Appeal No. 15 of 2023 of the District Court of Babati, originating in Civil Case No. 2 of 2023 of Mwada Primary Court)

1. JACKSON MREFU
2. MENYEE MREFU ................................................................. APPLICANTS
VERSUS
EVA MNYALO ....................................................................... RESPONDENT

RULING
J.R. KAHYOZA, J. (Delivered on 29 December 2023):

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Eva Mnyalo sued Jackson Mrefu and Menyee Mrefu in Mwada Primary Court for property destruction compensation and was awarded TZS 3,710,000. The applicants' appeal to the District Court of Babati was dismissed. They failed to appeal to the High Court in time, and their first application for extension of time (Misc. Civil Application No. 36 of 2023) was dismissed for want of prosecution on 23 November 2023 when applicants and counsel failed to appear. Instead of applying for restoration, they filed duplicate Application No. 39 of 2023. The High Court held that the fresh application was incompetent, misconceived, and an abuse of court process; the proper remedy was an application for restoration under Rule 17 principles of GN No. 312 of 1964. The application was struck out with costs.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- Eva Mnyalo sued Jackson Mrefu and Menyee Mrefu at Mwada Primary Court in Civil Case No. 2 of 2023 claiming TZS 7,420,000 for destruction of property and costs.
- Primary Court awarded Eva Mnyalo TZS 3,710,000.
- Applicants appealed to District Court of Babati in Misc. Civil Appeal No. 15 of 2023; appeal dismissed and judgment confirmed.
- Applicants failed to lodge intended High Court appeal on time.
- Filed Misc. Civil Application No. 36 of 2023 seeking extension of time under certificate of urgency.
- High Court scheduled hearing on 23 November 2023; neither applicants nor advocate appeared.
- High Court dismissed application for want of prosecution.
- Applicants then filed Misc. Civil Application No. 39 of 2023 seeking the same extension through the same advocate without seeking restoration.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Whether a fresh application for extension of time was competent after an earlier application seeking the same relief was dismissed for want of prosecution (No).
- Which law governs extension of time in matters originating from Primary Courts (Rule 3 of GN No. 312 of 1964; Law of Limitation Act held inapplicable).
- Whether applicants were required to seek restoration of the dismissed application (Yes, under Rule 17 principles).
- Whether filing duplicate identical application amounted to an abuse of court process (Yes).

4. LAWS CITED:
- Civil Procedure (Appeals in Proceedings Originating in Primary Courts) Rules, GN No. 312 of 1964, Rules 3 and 17.
- Law of Limitation Act [Cap. 89 R.E. 2019] (Inapplicable).

5. FINAL DECISION AND ORDERS:
1. Application Misc. Civil Application No. 39 of 2023 is STRUCK OUT.
2. Costs awarded against the applicants.`,
