      rawExtractedText: `IN THE COURT OF APPEAL OF TANZANIA AT DAR ES SALAAM
CIVIL APPEAL NO. 452 OF 2021 / [2023] TZCA 18021
(Arising from Tax Appeal No. 10 of 2020 of the Tax Revenue Appeals Tribunal at Dar es Salaam, originating from Customs and Excise Appeal No. 13 of 2018 of the Tax Revenue Appeals Board)

LAKAIRO INVESTMENT LIMITED ..................................................... APPELLANT
VERSUS
COMMISSIONER GENERAL, TANZANIA REVENUE AUTHORITY ............ RESPONDENT

JUDGMENT OF THE COURT
CORAM: A.G. MWARIJA, J.A.; P.M. KENTE, J.A.; Z.G. MURUKE, J.A.
(Hearing: 14 August 2023 | Judgment Prepared: 27 December 2023 | Delivered: 29 December 2023)

1. CASE SUMMARY:
TRA issued a tax demand dated 24 January 2017 for customs taxes/duties on export "exit" goods allegedly diverted into Tanzania's domestic market. Lakairo disputed the assessment, claiming EAC preferential treatment (0% duty) applied instead of Common External Tariff (25% duty). Lakairo filed a review application on 1 March 2017, after the mandatory 30-day period expired. The Commissioner did not determine the review within 30 days. The Board allowed the review to proceed reasoning that both parties defaulted. The Tribunal reversed, holding the late review was incompetent. The Court of Appeal affirmed the Tribunal, ruling that two wrongs do not make a right: the Commissioner's inaction under Section 229(4)/(5) EACCMA applies only to valid, timely applications and cannot cure a time-barred application. Lakairo's proper remedy was to seek an extension of time. Appeal dismissed with costs.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- TRA issued tax demand dated 24 January 2017 demanding customs duties and taxes on diverted exit goods.
- Note: Page 2 states demanded sum as TZS 4,067,188.39, while Page 10 states TZS 4,067,711,188.39.
- Lakairo claimed goods originated from Kenya and qualified for EAC preferential treatment (0% customs + 18% VAT). TRA applied CET rates (25% + 18% VAT).
- Lakairo filed an application for review to the Commissioner on 1 March 2017, outside the statutory 30-day window under Section 229(1) of EACCMA.
- The Commissioner did not determine the review within 30 days.
- TRAB entertained the review holding both parties were in breach of statutory deadlines.
- TRAT reversed TRAB on 25 June 2021, holding the review was time-barred and incompetent.
- Lakairo appealed to the Court of Appeal.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Whether the tax demand dated 24 January 2017 was the appealable tax decision.
- Whether the preliminary objection raised a pure point of law under Mukisa Biscuit.
- Whether Lakairo's review application was filed outside the mandatory 30 days under Section 229(1) of EACCMA.
- Whether the Commissioner's failure to determine the review within 30 days validated a late application ("two wrongs do not make a right").
- Whether the Board had jurisdiction to entertain a time-barred review.
- Whether Sections 50 and 53 of the Tax Administration Act assisted the appellant.

4. LAWS CITED:
- East African Community Customs Management Act, 2004 (EACCMA), Sections 229(1), 229(4) (noted typo reference "section 299(4)"), 229(5).
- Tax Administration Act, Cap. 438 R.E. 2019, Sections 50(3)(c)(ii), 53.

5. CASES CITED:
- Mukisa Biscuit Manufacturing Company Limited v West End Distributors Company Limited [1969] EA 696 (Preliminary objections on pure points of law).

6. FINAL ORDERS:
1. The appeal by Lakairo Investment Limited is DISMISSED with costs.
2. The decision of the Tax Revenue Appeals Tribunal is AFFIRMED.`,
