      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, DISTRICT REGISTRY OF ARUSHA
LAND APPEAL NO. 185 OF 2022 / [2023] TZHC 23540
(Arising from Land Application No. 08 of 2016 of the District Land and Housing Tribunal for Karatu)

RICHARD AMNAAY ................................................................. APPELLANT
VERSUS
PHILLIPO DAFFI LOLO (also cited as PHILIPO DAFFI LOLO) ........... RESPONDENT

JUDGMENT
J.C. TIGANGA, J. (Hearing: 22 November 2023 | Delivered: 29 December 2023)

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Richard Amnaay and Phillipo Daffi Lolo disputed ownership of approximately three acres of land at Endashangwet Village (Endashwangwet), Kati Hamlet, Karatu District. Amnaay claimed village allocation during Operesheni Vijiji in 1975 and 30+ years occupation. Lolo contended his father bought the land from Baha Hondi (Baahondi) in 1973 for three goats, his mother used it and gifted it to him in 1981. The District Land and Housing Tribunal decided for Lolo. On appeal, the High Court held that Amnaay failed to produce documentary proof of allocation, whereas Lolo tendered sale agreement D1 and gift document D2 without objection or cross-examination from Amnaay. His late claim of forgery was an afterthought; fraud must be strictly proved to a heightened standard of probability. Appeal dismissed; no order as to costs due to 15-year dispute duration.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- Disputed land measures ~3 acres at Endashangwet Village (Endashwangwet), Kati Hamlet, Karatu District.
- Amnaay claimed village allocation in 1975 under Operesheni Vijiji and continuous use.
- Amnaay alleged Lolo entered land in 2007 while Amnaay attended sick child in hospital.
- Lolo claimed father bought land from Baha Hondi (Baahondi) in 1973 for three goats; mother gifted land to Lolo in 1981.
- Lolo produced sale agreement Exhibit D1 and gift document Exhibit D2.
- Amnaay did not object to exhibits nor cross-examine Lolo on them at trial.
- DLHT for Karatu found for Lolo; Amnaay appealed.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Whether DLHT was justified in holding land belonged to Lolo.
- Which party carried burden of proof under Sections 110 & 111 of Evidence Act (balance of probabilities).
- Effect of failing to object to or cross-examine on documentary exhibits.
- Required standard of proof for civil allegations of fraud/forgery (heightened probability).

4. LAWS & PRECEDENTS CITED:
- Evidence Act, Cap. 6 R.E. 2019, Sections 110 & 111.
- Miller v Minister of Pensions [1937] 2 All ER 372 (Balance of probabilities).
- Paulina Samson Ndawavya v Theresia Thomas Madaha, CA No. 45 of 2017.
- Maria Amandus Kavishe v Norah Waziri Mzeru, CA No. 365 of 2019.
- Anthony Masanga v Penina Mama Ngesi, CA No. 118 of 2014.
- Hamza Byarushengo v Fulgencia Manya, CA No. 33 of 2017.
- Twazihirwa Abhaham Mgema v James Christian Basil, CA No. 229 of 2018.
- Ratilal Gordhanbhai Patel v Lalji Makanji [1957] EA 314 (Heightened standard for fraud in civil cases).
- Omari Yusuph v Rahma Ahmed Abdulkadir [1987] TLR 169.

5. FINAL DECISION & ORDERS:
1. Appeal by Richard Amnaay is DISMISSED.
2. DLHT decision in favour of Phillipo Daffi Lolo AFFIRMED.
3. No order as to costs (litigation having continued for over 15 years).`,
