      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, LABOUR DIVISION AT ARUSHA
LABOUR COMPLAINT NO. 04 OF 2013 / [2023] TZHC 23503

DR. RONILICK KASAMBARA MCHAMI .................................................... COMPLAINANT
VERSUS
1. BRIGHTON B.L. KILEWA
2. BISHOP PAULO ISAKI AKYOO
3. GOVERNING BOARD OF MAKUMIRA UNIVERSITY
4. REGISTERED TRUSTEES OF THE EVANGELICAL LUTHERAN
   CHURCH IN TANZANIA (ELCT) ..................................................... RESPONDENTS

JUDGMENT
J.C. TIGANGA, J. (Hearing: 27 November 2023 | Delivered: 29 December 2023)

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Dr. Ronilick Kasambara Mchami, an ordained ELCT pastor, theologian, lecturer, and advocate, claimed over TZS 400,000,000 in damages alleging that letters and oral announcements communicating the University Governing Board's decision of 23 January 2006 to stop his service at Makumira and return him to Iringa Diocese defamed him. The respondents maintained Mchami was on secondment, not terminated, and that communications were official, made in good faith, and protected by qualified privilege. The High Court dismissed the complaint, holding that parties are strictly bound by their pleadings (Order VI Rule 7 CPC) and settled Industrial Court determinations (Inquiry No. 145 of 2006) cannot be relitigated. The official letters sent to institutional officers were not defamatory, and oral announcements to staff and students were protected by qualified privilege since Bishop Akyoo acted under an administrative duty and malice was not proved. Complaint dismissed; no order as to costs.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- Mchami served as lecturer at Makumira Theological College (later Makumira University College of Tumaini University) from 1988.
- On 23 January 2006, Makumira Governing Board resolved to stop Mchami's services at Makumira ("kusitisha huduma yako") and return him to Iringa Diocese.
- Official letters (23 January 2006, 28 April 2006) were sent and copied to institutional leaders (Board Chairman, Provost, Vice Chancellor, Presiding Bishop, Bishop of Iringa).
- Bishop Paulo Isaki Akyoo announced the decision orally to Makumira lecturers, supporting staff, and students.
- Industrial Court Inquiry No. 145 of 2006 previously determined that Mchami was serving on secondment and returned to his home diocese rather than dismissed.
- Mchami instituted Labour Complaint No. 04 of 2013 claiming multi-million damages for defamation.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Whether the letters dated 23 January 2006 and 28 April 2006 were defamatory.
- Whether oral announcements to university staff and students were defamatory.
- Whether the communications were protected by qualified privilege.
- Whether complainant proved express malice.
- Whether court could determine unpleaded issues regarding board meeting legality or reopen settled employment determinations.

4. LAWS & PRECEDENTS CITED:
- Civil Procedure Code, Cap. 33 R.E. 2019, Order VI Rule 7.
- Judicature and Application of Laws Act, Cap. 358 R.E. 2019, Section 2(3).
- Universities Act No. 7 of 2005, Sections 43, 44(2)(d), 45.
- Evidence Act, Cap. 6 R.E. 2022, Sections 3(2)(b), 110, 111.
- Yara Tanzania Ltd v IKUWO General Enterprises Ltd, CA No. 309 of 2019 (Bound by pleadings).
- Barclays Bank Tanzania Ltd v Jacob Muro, CA No. 357 of 2019.
- James Funke Gwagilo v Attorney General [2004] TLR 161.
- Joseph Marco v Pascal Rweyemamu [1977] TLR 59.
- Khatibu Said Bondo v Juma Hamis Mbozwa, CA No. 10 of 2021 (Defamation elements).
- PSSSF v Siriel Mchembe, CA No. 126 of 2018.
- Ignatius N. Bashemela v Michael Peter Mpambagomba, CA No. 31 of 2016.
- Makori Wassaga v Joshua Mwaikambo [1987] TLR 88 (Qualified privilege).
- Astus Njare Masule v Goddani Lunala [2002] TLR 196.
- Future Century Ltd v TANESCO [2016] TLR 277.

5. FINAL ORDERS:
1. The three letters were not defamatory in their natural meaning.
2. Oral statements to staff and students were protected by qualified privilege.
3. Malice was not proved.
4. Labour Complaint No. 04 of 2013 is DISMISSED in its entirety.
5. No order as to costs (labour complaint).`,
