      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, DISTRICT REGISTRY OF ARUSHA
LAND APPEAL NO. 106 OF 2022 / [2023] TZHC 23679
(Arising from Application No. 119 of 2023 of the District Land and Housing Tribunal of Babati)

1. SAFARI ARRA (also cited as SAFARI ARA)
2. MOGUSHA JOSEPH ............................................................. APPELLANTS
VERSUS
PAULO YAKOBO .................................................................. RESPONDENT

JUDGMENT
MOHAMED R. GWAE, J. (Hearing: 26 October 2023 | Delivered: 22 December 2023)

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Safari Arra sold approximately nine acres at Magara Village to Paulo Yakobo in 2008 (witnessed by Magara VEO Husseni Hatibu). In 2009, Safari purported to sell six acres at Maweni Village to Mogusha Joseph. Although the agreements mentioned different acreage and village names, both recorded identical neighbouring boundaries (Jerome Ngalawa on East, Juma Ramadhani on West, Magdalena on South). The High Court held that the agreements concerned the same land. Under the priority principle (Ombeni Kimaro v Joseph Mishili), Paulo was first in time, and Safari had completely divested himself of title, leaving no valid interest to convey to Mogusha. Appeal dismissed with costs payable by Safari Arra; DLHT order requiring Safari to compensate Mogusha preserved.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- 2008: Safari Arra sold 9 acres at Magara Village to Paulo Yakobo under written agreement witnessed by Magara VEO.
- 25 June 2009: Safari purported to sell 6 acres at Maweni Village to Mogusha Joseph.
- 2012: Appellants entered and cultivated the land.
- Both agreements were admitted by DLHT Babati; matching boundary abuttals proved identity of land (East: Jerome Ngalawa, West: Juma Ramadhani, South: Magdalena).
- DLHT declared Paulo lawful owner and ordered Safari to compensate Mogusha.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Whether DLHT was justified in declaring Paulo Yakobo lawful owner.
- Whether 2008 and 2009 agreements concerned the same parcel.
- Which purchaser held superior legal interest under the priority principle.
- Whether omission to visit locus in quo prejudiced the case.

4. PRECEDENTS CITED:
- Ombeni Kimaro v Joseph Mishili t/a Catholic Charismatic Renewal, CA No. 33 of 2017 (Court of Appeal of Tanzania: earlier purchaser in time holds superior legal title in double sale from same vendor).

5. FINAL ORDERS:
1. Both agreements concerned the same property.
2. Paulo Yakobo purchased first in time and holds superior legal interest.
3. Safari Arra had no valid interest remaining to transfer to Mogusha Joseph in 2009.
4. Appeal is DISMISSED.
5. Safari Arra ordered to pay costs in High Court and DLHT.
6. DLHT compensation order in favour of Mogusha against Safari remains in force.`,
