      fileSize: '390 KB',
      fileType: 'PDF',
      rawExtractedText: `IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA, IRINGA SUB-REGISTRY
CRIMINAL APPEAL NO. 51 OF 2023 / [2023] TZHC 23507
(Arising from Criminal Case No. 21 of 2021 of the District Court of Mufindi at Mafinga)

THEOBAT LAMECK MLYUKA (also cited as THEOBAT MLYUKA) ............... APPELLANT
VERSUS
REPUBLIC .................................................................... RESPONDENT

JUDGMENT
I.C. MUGETA, J. (Hearing: 22 November 2023 | Delivered: 29 December 2023)

1. CASE SUMMARY:
Theobat Lameck Mlyuka was convicted of two counts of rape of his 11-year-old granddaughter and sentenced to concurrent 30-year prison terms. The High Court accepted proof of age and anatomical penetration, but allowed the appeal because the prosecution failed to prove beyond reasonable doubt that the appellant was the perpetrator. Material contradictions surrounded how the offences were discovered: the victim claimed reporting to her grandmother and teacher, whereas the grandmother said the child only disclosed upon questioning after noticing a foul smell and the teacher received a tip about village rumours. The examining doctor observed no foul smell, the chain of reporting/investigation was missing, and the trial magistrate failed to ask neutral clarifying questions when the unrepresented accused and his defence witness testified. Convictions quashed; sentences set aside; release ordered.

2. MATERIAL FACTS:
- Appellant (~62 years old) lived at Ihegela Village, Mufindi District; victim was his 11-year-old granddaughter.
- Two rape incidents alleged: one inside a house in the evening and another in a field during ulanzi harvesting.
- Victim (PW1) said she reported to grandmother (PW2) and teacher (PW3).
- PW2 stated the child never reported until questioned due to a foul smell; PW3 stated she learned through a telephone tip about circulating village rumours.
- Medical examination on 31 March 2022 confirmed absence of hymen and discharge, but the doctor did not detect the foul smell.
- District Court of Mufindi at Mafinga convicted the appellant on both counts and sentenced him to concurrent 30-year terms.

3. LEGAL ISSUES:
- Whether the prosecution proved beyond reasonable doubt that the appellant was the perpetrator.
- Whether proof of penetration and age equals proof of offender identity.
- Whether contradictions between PW1, PW2, and PW3 created reasonable doubt.
- Whether the medical evidence supported the alleged circumstances of discovery.
- Application of Section 143 of the Evidence Act regarding the missing chain of evidence.

4. LAWS CITED:
- Evidence Act, Cap. 6 R.E. 2022, Section 143 (Number of witnesses).
- Note: No judicial precedents were cited by name in the judgment.

5. FINAL ORDERS:
1. Criminal Appeal No. 51 of 2023 is ALLOWED.
2. Both convictions for rape are QUASHED.
3. The two concurrent sentences of 30 years' imprisonment are SET ASIDE.
4. The appellant is to be RELEASED from prison immediately unless lawfully held for another offence.`,
