      docType: 'Judgment',
      court: 'High Court of Tanzania (Temeke Sub-Registry, One Stop Judicial Centre)',
      citation: '[2024] TZHC 11172',
      date: '2024-12-31',
      pdfFileName: 'Diana Ferdinandi Makiluli vs Diana Ferdanand Makiluli(As an administratrix of the estate of the late   Enock Mnkande Rabieth ) 2024 TZHC 11172 (31 December 2024).pdf',
      downloadUrl: 'https://tanzlii.org/tz/judgment/high-court-tanzania/2024/11172/download/pdf',
      extractedText: 'IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA AT TEMEKE SUB-REGISTRY ONE STOP JUDICIAL CENTRE AT TEMEKE. MATRIMONIAL APPEAL NO. 24129 OF 2024 (Arising from Matrimonial Cause No. 288 of 2023 of the District Court of Temeke at Temeke). DIANA FERDINAND MAKILULI VERSUS DIANA FERDINAND MAKILULI, AS ADMINISTRATRIX OF THE ESTATE OF THE LATE ENOCK MNKANDE RABIETH. JUDGMENT. S.D. MWAIPOPO, J.: Diana Ferdinand Makiluli petitioned for divorce against her husband Enock Mnkande Rabieth in Matrimonial Cause No. 288 of 2023. Following conciliation failure before the Msasani Ward Marriage Conciliation Board (Exhibit P1), the parties agreed on irretrievable breakdown and executed a comprehensive Deed of Settlement settling child custody, maintenance (TZS 600,000 monthly, school fees, uniforms, medical care), access, and transferring matrimonial assets to their three children (Alvin, Rovin, and Shavin). The hearing concluded and the deed was filed on 8 February 2024. After the hearing concluded, Enock allegedly passed away in July 2024. On 15 August 2024, the District Court (Hon. R.E. Mwaisaka, PRM) delivered judgment dissolving the marriage and adopting the settlement deed. Diana appealed on the sole ground that the trial court erred in delivering judgment after Enock had died and after the court had allegedly been notified. Ms. Banana Hussein for the appellant argued that death automatically dissolves marriage, the divorce cause abated, and Order XXII Rule 6 of the Civil Procedure Code does not apply to personal matrimonial causes. The respondent as administratrix did not oppose the appeal. HELD: (1) An examination of the electronic case management system entries for 12 July 2024 and 15 August 2024 reveals no record or documentary proof that the District Court was notified of the death prior to delivery of judgment. Death is a question of fact that must be proved by evidence on record (Amratlal D.M. v A.H. Jariwala). (2) Section 2 of the Civil Procedure Code applies to fill procedural gaps in the Law of Marriage Act. Under Order XXII Rule 6 of the Civil Procedure Code, where a party dies between the conclusion of the hearing and the pronouncement of judgment, the proceedings do not abate, and the court is legally empowered to deliver judgment with the same force as if pronounced before the death occurred. The hearing having fully concluded and only delivery of judgment remaining, the District Court was legally entitled to pronounce judgment and adopt the settlement. Appeal dismissed without costs.',
      passages: [
        {
          id: 'pass-049-1',
          passageIndex: 1,
          text: 'MATRIMONIAL APPEAL & UNUSUAL REPRESENTATIVE CAPACITY: Matrimonial Appeal No. 24129 of 2024 arising from Temeke District Court Matrimonial Cause No. 288 of 2023. Appellant Diana Ferdinand Makiluli sued herself in her capacity as administratrix of the estate of her late husband Enock Mnkande Rabieth, challenging the validity of a divorce judgment delivered after the husband’s death.',
          wordCount: 57,
          pageNumber: 1
        },
        {
          id: 'pass-049-2',
          passageIndex: 2,
          text: 'HELD BY S.D. MWAIPOPO, J.: Death is a question of fact that must be substantiated by evidence and reflected in the court record; the electronic records showed no proof of death notification prior to judgment. Furthermore, by virtue of Section 2 of the Civil Procedure Code, Order XXII Rule 6 applies to fill the procedural gap in the Law of Marriage Act. Where death occurs after the conclusion of hearing and submission of a signed settlement deed, the matrimonial cause does not abate, and the trial court possesses full legal competence to pronounce judgment and adopt the settlement terms.',
          wordCount: 99,
          pageNumber: 8
        },
        {
          id: 'pass-049-3',
          passageIndex: 3,
          text: 'FINAL OPERATIVE ORDERS: The High Court dismissed the appeal in its entirety for lack of merit. The judgment and decree of the District Court of Temeke adopting the Deed of Settlement remains valid and in force. No order as to costs. Right of further appeal explained.',
          wordCount: 47,
          pageNumber: 11
        }
      ]
