      downloadUrl: 'https://tanzlii.org/tz/judgment/high-court-tanzania/2025/8662/download/pdf',
      extractedText: 'IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA IN THE SUB-REGISTRY OF GEITA AT GEITA. CRIMINAL APPEAL NO. 28368 OF 2025 (Arising from District Court of Nyang’hwale Criminal Case No. 22410 of 2024 / 2025). DAUDI JEREMIA HUSSEIN VERSUS REPUBLIC. JUDGMENT. G.V. MWAKAPEJE, J.: The appellant Daudi Jeremia Hussein was convicted by the District Court of Nyang’hwale of rape under Section 130(1), 130(2)(e), and 131(1) and abduction under Section 134 of the Penal Code [Cap. 16 R.E. 2023] and sentenced to 36 years’ imprisonment. The allegations arose from events between 21 and 23 August 2025 involving teenage girls ABC (17), DEF (17), GHK (14), and XYZ (15). Complainant ABC alleged the appellant posed as a teacher, promised marriage, and had sexual intercourse with her twice in Room 14 of Kabeta Guest House. Appellant, appearing in person, contended that no penetration was proved, the medical examination showed no fresh injuries, the guest-house register (Exhibit P5) named two men (Daud Jelemia and Yusuph Katoto) without mentioning ABC, DEF was his wife, and movements were voluntary among relatives. State Attorney Kabula Benjamine argued that ABC gave direct evidence of penetration and the charges were proved beyond reasonable doubt. HELD: (1) Under Section 3(2)(a) Evidence Act and Mohamed Haji Ali v DPP, the prosecution bears the unshifting burden of proof beyond reasonable doubt. (2) While rape can be proved by credible victim testimony without medical corroboration (Goodluck Kyando v Republic), ABC’s evidence lacked coherence regarding resistance, struggle, or alarm, and medical findings of non-virginity without fresh trauma or semen could not establish penetration by the appellant (Issaya Renatus v Republic, Selemani Makumba v Republic). (3) The guest-house register (Exhibit P5) listed two adult men and did not establish exclusive occupation or corroborate ABC’s presence. (4) Unexplained failure to call crucial eyewitness DEF warranted an adverse inference against the prosecution (Buruhani Selemani Mbombo v Republic). (5) Abduction under Section 134 requires proof of unlawful taking from custody against parental will (Elias Nduli v Republic); GHK left with her father’s knowledge to visit her grandmother, XYZ followed voluntarily, and open movements among village relatives negated criminal taking. Appeal allowed; convictions and 36-year sentence quashed; immediate release ordered unless lawfully detained.',
