      downloadUrl: 'https://tanzlii.org/tz/judgment/high-court-tanzania/2025/8706/download/pdf',
      extractedText: 'IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA IN THE SUB-REGISTRY OF MOSHI AT MOSHI. CRIMINAL APPEAL NO. 30 OF 2021 (Arising from Criminal Case No. 104 of 2019 of the District Court of Siha at Siha, reheard pursuant to Court of Appeal Criminal Appeal No. 509 of 2021 [2024] TZCA 1156). DEOGRATIUS PETER SHAYO VERSUS REPUBLIC. JUDGMENT. A.P. KILIMI, J.: The appellant Deogratius Peter Shayo was convicted by the District Court of Siha on his own plea of guilty of rape under Section 130(1), 130(2)(b), and 131(1) of the Penal Code [Cap. 16 R.E. 2023] and sentenced to 30 years’ imprisonment. The offence occurred on 7 November 2019 in Lawate, Siha, where the appellant forcefully undressed and penetrated his co-worker complainant without consent. Following remittal by the Court of Appeal for proper rehearing, appellant appeared in person and contended that his plea was equivocal, he did not understand the consequences, the PF3 and caution statement were not read aloud, and police promised him a conditional discharge. Senior State Attorney Grace Kabu maintained that the plea strictly complied with Section 245(1) & (2) CPA and an appeal against conviction was barred under Section 360(1) CPA. HELD: (1) Under Paskali Kamara v Republic [2022] TZCA 631, the plea was unequivocal; the appellant stated "It is true" upon the charge being read and gave a detailed admission of forcefully undressing, penetrating without consent, and ejaculating upon the facts being narrated. (2) Unrepresented status does not invalidate a voluntary plea where the accusation was fully explained and comprehended. (3) Omission to read the PF3 and caution statement aloud after admission to form part of the proceedings caused no prejudice because the conviction rested upon the appellant’s own complete factual admissions (Mathias Barua v Republic). (4) Under Section 360(1) CPA, an appeal against conviction following an unequivocal plea of guilty is barred. Appeal dismissed; conviction and 30-year sentence affirmed.',
