      date: '2025-12-29',
      pdfFileName: 'Peter Thomas Bocco vs Republic 2025 TZHC 8732 (29 December 2025).pdf',
      downloadUrl: 'https://tanzlii.org/tz/judgment/high-court-tanzania/2025/8732/download/pdf',
      extractedText: 'IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA IN THE SUB-REGISTRY OF TANGA AT TANGA. DC CRIMINAL REVISION NO. 000006375 OF 2025 (Arising from Criminal Case No. 01 of 2023 / 6375 of 2025 of the District Court of Korogwe at Korogwe). PETER THOMAS BOCCO VERSUS REPUBLIC. RULING. M.J. CHABA, J.: The applicant Peter Thomas Bocco was undergoing criminal trial before the District Court of Korogwe on four counts involving stealing by agent and forgery. During the cross-examination of forensic document and handwriting expert SSP Maria Tryphon Njenga (PW7) on 6 March 2025, the trial magistrate (Hon. J. Matinde, PRM) recorded disconnected, monosyllabic answers ("It is correct", "I don’t know", "Yes", "No") without recording the questions or context, and refused an unopposed defence adjournment requested due to counsel’s fatigue and illness, closing cross-examination. Advocate Yona Lucas for the applicant sought criminal revision under Section 372(1) of the Criminal Procedure Act [Cap. 20 R.E. 2023]. State Attorney Sarah Daudi Wangwe opposed revision on grounds that interlocutory proceedings cannot be revised under Section 394(1) CPA and no bias was proved. HELD: (1) Under Section 372(1) CPA, the High Court has revisional jurisdiction to examine the correctness, legality, propriety, and regularity of ongoing subordinate-court proceedings prior to final judgment where fundamental defects arise. (2) Allegations of judicial bias and active restriction were not proved by cogent evidence (Athumani Msumi v Mohamed Athumani Msumi, Mwesigwa Zaidi Siraji v Mara Textile Ltd). (3) Under Mangalu Maduhu v Republic, where questions are not separately recorded, evidence must be transcribed in narrative form to preserve the substance and context of both question and answer. Recording isolated answers rendered the cross-examination unintelligible and deprived both the trial court and appellate courts of a reviewable record. (4) Proceedings of 6 March 2025 nullified and expunged; PW7 cross-examination ordered to restart afresh. Recusal was left to be moved before the trial court. Charges not dismissed.',
