      pdfFileName: 'Rogath K Katende vs CRDB Bank PLC and Others 2025 TZHC 8843 (29 December 2025).pdf',
      downloadUrl: 'https://tanzlii.org/tz/judgment/high-court-tanzania/2025/8843/download/pdf',
      extractedText: 'IN THE HIGH COURT OF TANZANIA IN THE SUB-REGISTRY OF DODOMA AT DODOMA. MISCELLANEOUS CIVIL APPLICATION NO. 7327 OF 2025 (Arising from Land Appeal No. 89 of 2022 / Land Application No. 107 of 2020). ROGATH K. KATENDE VERSUS CRDB BANK PLC, CITY LAND COMPANY LTD AND ABDALLAH ABUBAKAR SAGGAF. RULING. I.D. MUSOKWA, J.: The applicant Rogath K. Katende applied for extension of time under Section 11(1) of the Appellate Jurisdiction Act [Cap. 141 R.E. 2019], Section 14(1) of the Law of Limitation Act [Cap. 89 R.E. 2019], and Section 93 CPC to file a notice of appeal against the decision in Land Appeal No. 89 of 2022 delivered on 17 November 2023. The applicant had previously filed an appeal before the Court of Appeal, which was struck out on 18 March 2025 due to procedural anomalies in the record of appeal. The applicant filed this application on 2 April 2025, 15 days post-ruling. CRDB Bank PLC, City Land Company Ltd, and Abdallah Abubakar Saggaf opposed the application, contending that the applicant failed to account for every single day of delay and that the prayer did not explicitly request an extension for serving the letter requesting copies of proceedings/judgment under Rule 84(1) Court of Appeal Rules. Advocate Sosthenes Mselingwa for the applicant contended that time spent prosecuting the struck-out appeal constitutes technical delay and sufficient cause. HELD: (1) Under Fortunatus Masha v William Shija and Mobrama Gold Corp v Minister for Energy, time spent actively prosecuting an appeal that is struck out for technicalities in the record constitutes technical delay and sufficient cause. (2) The 15-day interval between the Court of Appeal decision (18 March 2025) and filing this application (2 April 2025) was under a month, not inordinate, and established diligence (Hamisi Mohamed v Mtumwa Moshi). (3) The principle of accounting for every day is a prudential standard evaluating substantial overall explanation rather than requiring a rigid mathematical daily ledger. (4) The extension covers both filing/serving the notice of appeal (Rule 83) and serving the letter requesting copies under Rule 84(1). Application granted; 21 days allowed from 29 December 2025. No order as to costs.',
